window.ECONOMICS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"nature-concepts","old":{"title":"Nature of economics, basic concepts and economic systems","body":"Economics is the study of how people use scarce resources to satisfy their unlimited wants. Lionel Robbins (1932) defined it as \"the science which studies human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means which have alternative uses\". Adam Smith, whose \"Wealth of Nations\" (1776) earned him the title \"father of economics\", saw it as the study of the wealth of nations.\n\nBasic concepts:\n• Wants: human desires for goods and services; they are unlimited and recurring.\n• Scarcity: resources (land, labour, capital, entrepreneur) are limited relative to wants. Scarcity is the central problem of economics.\n• Choice: because of scarcity we must choose which wants to satisfy.\n• Scale of preference: a list of wants arranged in order of importance, so the most pressing are met first.\n• Opportunity cost: the next best alternative forgone. If a farmer uses his land for cassava instead of yam, the yam he could have grown is the opportunity cost. It is \"real cost\", not a money cost.\n• Free goods (air, sunlight) have no opportunity cost; economic goods are scarce and command a price.\n• Production possibility curve (PPC): shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources fully used. Points inside = unemployment or waste; outside = unattainable now; outward shift = growth.\n\nThe basic economic problems every society must answer: what to produce, how to produce, for whom to produce, and how efficiently resources are used and whether they will grow.\n\nEconomic systems:\n• Capitalist (free market, laissez-faire): private ownership of resources, the profit motive, the price mechanism (\"invisible hand\") and consumer sovereignty decide what is produced. Merits: efficiency, choice, initiative. Demerits: inequality, monopoly, neglect of public goods.\n• Socialist (command, centrally planned): the state owns the major means of production and a planning authority decides output and prices (the former USSR; North Korea today). Merits: less inequality, social welfare. Demerits: bureaucracy, little incentive, shortages.\n• Mixed economy: private and public sectors coexist, and government corrects market failures. Nigeria is a mixed economy: private firms such as Dangote Group work alongside government agencies and enterprises.\n• Some writers also list the traditional (subsistence) economy.\n\nEconomics as a science: it uses positive statements (what is: \"inflation is 20%\"), which can be tested, and normative statements (what ought to be: \"fuel subsidy should return\"), which are value judgements. Microeconomics studies individual units (a household, a firm, a market); macroeconomics studies the whole economy (national income, inflation, unemployment). \"Ceteris paribus\" means other things being equal.\n\nMarket failure terms JAMB uses: public goods (non-rival, non-excludable), merit goods (underconsumed), demerit goods (overconsumed), externalities (costs or benefits to third parties, e.g. gas flaring).\n\nCommon traps:\n• Opportunity cost is the NEXT BEST alternative only, not all alternatives and not the money price.\n• Scarcity is relative to wants: a rich country still faces scarcity.\n• Consumer sovereignty belongs to the capitalist system, not the socialist one.\n• Micro = small units; macro = aggregates. National income is macro.\n• A point inside the PPC shows unemployed resources, not impossible output."},"new":{"title":"Nature of economics, basic concepts and economic systems","body":"NATURE OF ECONOMICS — THINK LIKE AN ECONOMIST\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nEvery economy faces a problem: people want more than the available resources can provide. Economics studies how individuals, firms and governments make choices under that constraint. JAMB often tests not just definitions, but the difference between closely related concepts.\n\n1. WHAT ECONOMICS STUDIES\nLionel Robbins (1932) defined economics as the study of human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means that have alternative uses. Unpack this:\n• Ends = wants or objectives, such as food, education and healthcare.\n• Scarce means = limited money, time, land, labour and other resources.\n• Alternative uses = the same resource can serve different purposes.\n• Choice = selecting one use means giving up another.\n\nAdam Smith published The Wealth of Nations in 1776 and is conventionally called the father of modern economics. His work emphasised production, trade and national wealth. Robbins's definition instead highlights scarcity and choice.\n\nEXAMPLE: A student has ₦5,000 and needs both a textbook and transport. Buying the textbook may mean walking to school. The problem is not simply poverty: it is that available resources cannot satisfy every want simultaneously.\n\n2. SCARCITY, SHORTAGE AND POVERTY ARE NOT SYNONYMS\n• Scarcity: the universal condition of limited resources relative to wants. It exists even in rich countries.\n• Shortage: quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price; it can be temporary.\n• Poverty: insufficient income or resources to meet a defined standard of living.\n\nA fuel queue caused by a price ceiling or disrupted supply is a shortage. The general need to choose between roads, schools and hospitals reflects scarcity.\n\n3. BASIC ECONOMIC CONCEPTS\n• Wants: desires for goods and services; many recur after satisfaction.\n• Needs: essentials for basic survival and well-being; the boundary may vary by context.\n• Scale of preference: a ranked list of wants according to priority.\n• Choice: selecting among competing alternatives.\n• Opportunity cost: the value of the NEXT BEST alternative forgone, not the sum of everything rejected.\n• Resources/factors of production: land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship.\n• Efficiency: obtaining maximum feasible output from available resources.\n• Equity: fairness in the distribution of economic benefits and burdens.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A local council can build either a clinic, a market or a sports ground. It chooses the clinic; the market was its second choice. The opportunity cost is the market, not the market plus the sports ground.\n\n4. THE THREE CENTRAL ECONOMIC QUESTIONS\nEvery economic system must decide:\n• What to produce? For example, more rice or more industrial machinery?\n• How to produce? Labour-intensive methods or capital-intensive machinery?\n• For whom to produce? Who receives the output and on what basis?\n\nA fourth useful question is how to promote growth and adapt production over time.\n\n5. PRODUCTION POSSIBILITY CURVE (PPC)\nA PPC shows the maximum combinations of TWO goods an economy can produce with given resources and technology, assuming efficient use.\n• On the curve: productively efficient.\n• Inside the curve: feasible but inefficient, such as unemployed labour or idle machines.\n• Outside the curve: unattainable with current capacity.\n• Movement along the curve: more of one good requires less of the other.\n• Outward shift: growth in resources, technology or productivity.\n• Inward shift: loss of productive capacity, for example severe destruction of capital.\n\nWHY IS A TYPICAL PPC BOWED OUT? Resources are not equally suited to every use. As production shifts towards one good, increasing quantities of the other must be sacrificed: increasing opportunity cost. A straight PPC represents constant opportunity cost.\n\nEXAMPLE: If Nigeria reallocates workers and machines from textiles to food production, more food may mean fewer textiles. Improved farming technology can shift the feasible frontier outward.\n\n6. MICROECONOMICS VS MACROECONOMICS\n• Microeconomics studies individual consumers, firms, industries and specific markets: the price of garri, a firm's output, household spending.\n• Macroeconomics studies economy-wide aggregates: inflation, unemployment, GDP, economic growth and national income.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A question about the price of petrol in a specific market is generally microeconomic; a question about the overall inflation rate is macroeconomic.\n\n7. POSITIVE VS NORMATIVE ECONOMICS\n• Positive statement: describes or predicts what is; evidence can test it. Example: “A higher tax on a product may reduce purchases, other things equal.”\n• Normative statement: expresses what ought to be; it involves a value judgement. Example: “Government should make university education free.”\n\nThe word “should” is often a clue, but identify the underlying value judgement rather than relying on one word.\n\n8. ECONOMIC SYSTEMS — WHO MAKES THE DECISIONS?\n• Traditional: custom and inherited practices guide production and distribution.\n• Market/capitalist: private ownership and prices guide many decisions through demand and supply.\n• Command/socialist planned: public authorities centrally direct major production and allocation decisions.\n• Mixed: markets and government both play important roles; most contemporary economies, including Nigeria, combine them.\n\nDO NOT CONFUSE: A mixed economy is not an equal 50–50 split between state and private ownership. It simply combines market mechanisms with public intervention.\n\n9. WHY GOVERNMENTS INTERVENE\nMarkets may underprovide public goods, ignore external costs such as pollution, permit monopoly power or produce unequal outcomes. Governments use taxes, subsidies, regulation and public services. Intervention can also be inefficient, so economists compare costs and benefits.\n\nQUICK COMPARISONS\nScarcity ≠ shortage.\nOpportunity cost ≠ money spent.\nPPC inside ≠ impossible; PPC outside = currently unattainable.\nMicro = individual market; macro = economy-wide.\nPositive = testable claim; normative = value judgement.\nMarket economy ≠ absence of all government.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — ECONOMIC REASONING IN REAL LIFE\n\n10. FACTORS OF PRODUCTION AND THEIR REWARDS\n• Land includes natural resources such as farmland, forests, water and minerals; its conventional reward is rent.\n• Labour means human physical and mental effort; its reward is wages or salaries.\n• Capital means man-made productive assets such as machinery, tools and factory buildings; its reward is interest in the conventional factor-reward classification.\n• Entrepreneurship coordinates production, takes business risks and innovates; its reward is profit.\n\nIMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Money used to purchase a machine is financial capital; the machine itself is physical capital. A bank deposit is not itself a machine or factory. In everyday language “capital” may mean money, but exam questions often mean produced means of production.\n\n11. PRODUCTION, CONSUMPTION AND DISTRIBUTION\nProduction creates utility by making goods or services available in a useful form, place or time. Consumption uses goods and services to satisfy wants. Distribution concerns how output and income are shared among people and factors of production.\n\nA farmer grows cassava (production), a transporter moves it to a market (place utility), a processor makes garri (form utility), and a household eats it (consumption). The activities are linked, not separate economic worlds.\n\n12. ECONOMIC GOODS, FREE GOODS AND PUBLIC GOODS\nAn economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost. A free good, in the textbook sense, is available without scarcity in the relevant setting, such as abundant sunlight. A “free” school meal funded by taxes is NOT a free good economically: resources were used to provide it.\n\nPublic goods are generally non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. They are not defined by being government-owned or having a zero price. A public hospital is a publicly provided service, but an individual hospital bed is rival in consumption.\n\n13. THE PPC AS A STORY ABOUT TRADE-OFFS\nSuppose an economy can produce either 100 units of food and 0 machines, or 80 food and 10 machines, or 50 food and 20 machines.\n• First 10 machines cost 20 food units.\n• Next 10 machines cost 30 food units.\nThe opportunity cost of additional machines increases, illustrating a bowed-out PPC.\n\nAn economy can move from an interior point toward its frontier by employing idle resources more efficiently WITHOUT new technology. A frontier shifts outward only when productive capacity improves. JAMB often tests this distinction.\n\n14. SHORT RUN VS LONG RUN\nThe short run is a period in which at least one input is fixed; the long run is a period in which all inputs can be varied. These are economic definitions, not fixed numbers of days. A bakery may add workers quickly but need much longer to build another factory.\n\n15. HOW TO ANSWER A JAMB SCENARIO\nIf a question says “The government must choose between constructing a bridge and a hospital,” identify scarcity and opportunity cost.\nIf it says “Factories have unused machines and unemployed workers,” think a point INSIDE the PPC.\nIf it says “A new technology doubles output with the same inputs,” think an OUTWARD PPC shift.\nIf it says “Should government raise the minimum wage?”, think normative judgement.\n\nCHECK YOUR REASONING\nA government spends ₦50 million on a new clinic rather than repairing a road. Is the opportunity cost ₦50 million? Not necessarily. The opportunity cost is the value of the next-best forgone use of those resources, which may be the road repair. Monetary expenditure and opportunity cost answer different questions.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Can a billionaire experience scarcity? YES. Money and time remain limited relative to possible wants.\n2. A student chooses A over B and C; B was second best. What is the opportunity cost? B.\n3. What does a point inside the PPC show? Unused or inefficiently employed resources.\n4. What shifts a PPC outward? Greater productive capacity, such as improved technology.\n5. “Government ought to subsidise transport.” Positive or normative? Normative.\n6. Is Nigeria purely capitalist or purely centrally planned? Neither; it operates a mixed economy."}},{"id":"tools-analysis","old":{"title":"Tools of economic analysis","body":"Economists present and analyse data with tables, graphs, charts and simple statistics.\n\nPresenting data:\n• Tables: rows and columns, e.g. a demand schedule.\n• Graphs: a line graph plots one variable against another (price on the vertical axis, quantity on the horizontal in demand and supply). The slope shows the rate of change.\n• Bar chart: bars of equal width, height proportional to value; component (subdivided) bar charts show parts of a total.\n• Pie chart: a circle divided into sectors. Angle of a sector = (item ÷ total) × 360°.\n• Histogram: bars for grouped (continuous) data with no gaps; the tallest bar is the modal class.\n• Pictogram: pictures, each standing for a fixed quantity.\n\nMeasures of central tendency:\n• Mean (arithmetic average) = sum of values ÷ number of values: x̄ = Σx ÷ n. For a frequency table, x̄ = Σfx ÷ Σf.\n• Median: the middle value when the data are arranged in order. For n items, position = (n + 1) ÷ 2.\n• Mode: the value that occurs most often. Data can be bimodal.\n\nWorked example: the weekly sales (₦'000) of a kiosk are 12, 15, 9, 15, 20, 11, 16.\nMean = (12 + 15 + 9 + 15 + 20 + 11 + 16) ÷ 7 = 98 ÷ 7 = 14, i.e. ₦14,000.\nOrdered: 9, 11, 12, 15, 15, 16, 20; median = 4th value = 15 (₦15,000).\nMode = 15 (₦15,000). Range = 20 − 9 = 11 (₦11,000).\n\nPie chart example: a family spends ₦30,000 on food out of ₦120,000: angle = (30,000 ÷ 120,000) × 360° = 90°.\n\nMeasures of dispersion (spread):\n• Range = highest − lowest value.\n• Mean deviation = Σ|x − x̄| ÷ n.\n• Variance = Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n; standard deviation = √variance.\nExample: 2, 4, 6. Mean = 4; deviations −2, 0, 2; squares 4, 0, 4; variance = 8 ÷ 3 = 2.67; standard deviation = √2.67 ≈ 1.63; mean deviation = (2 + 0 + 2) ÷ 3 = 1.33.\n\nIndex numbers compare a value with a base year (= 100):\n• Simple price index = (current price ÷ base-year price) × 100.\nExample: a bag of rice costs ₦40,000 in the base year and ₦52,000 now: index = (52,000 ÷ 40,000) × 100 = 130, a 30% rise.\n• Weighted index: Σ(price relative × weight) ÷ Σweights. The Consumer Price Index is a weighted index; the NBS uses it to measure inflation.\n\nMerits of the mean: uses all values; demerit: distorted by extreme values. The median and mode are not affected by extremes.\n\nCommon traps:\n• In the median, arrange the figures in order FIRST.\n• Pie chart angles add up to 360°, not 100.\n• An index of 130 means a 30% rise, not 130%.\n• The modal class in a histogram is the tallest bar, not the widest interval or the middle one.\n• Use the correct base: percentage change = (new − old) ÷ old × 100."},"new":{"title":"Tools of economic analysis","body":"TOOLS OF ECONOMIC ANALYSIS — READ, CALCULATE AND INTERPRET\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB uses tables, graphs, percentages, averages and index numbers to test whether you can reason from data. Learn the meaning of each tool and practise the calculations.\n\n1. TABLES AND SCHEDULES\nA table organises observations in rows and columns. A demand schedule, for example, may list the quantities consumers buy at different prices.\n\nEXAMPLE — DEMAND SCHEDULE\nPrice (₦):          100 | 200 | 300\nQuantity demanded:   30 |  20 |  10\n\nAs price rises, quantity demanded falls, other things being equal. A table shows the numerical relationship; a graph makes its direction easier to see.\n\n2. GRAPHS: WHAT THE AXES MEAN\nFor conventional economics demand and supply diagrams:\n• Vertical (Y) axis = price.\n• Horizontal (X) axis = quantity.\n• Downward-sloping demand curve = inverse relationship.\n• Upward-sloping supply curve = direct relationship, other things equal.\n\nA movement ALONG a curve occurs when the good's own price changes. A SHIFT of the whole curve reflects another determinant, such as income, tastes or production costs.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Do not call a rise in quantity demanded an “increase in demand” when the cause is only a lower own price.\n\n3. CHOOSING THE RIGHT CHART\n• Bar chart: compares separate categories such as output across states. Bars usually have gaps.\n• Histogram: shows frequencies for continuous grouped data; bars touch. With unequal class widths, use frequency density for bar heights.\n• Pie chart: shows each category's share of a whole; all angles sum to 360°.\n• Line graph: displays trends over time or relationships between variables.\n• Frequency table: shows how often each observation or interval occurs.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE — PIE CHART\nA household spends 15% of its budget on transport.\nSector angle = (15 ÷ 100) × 360° = 54°.\nIf food accounts for 40%, its angle = 144°.\nCheck: all category percentages must sum to 100%.\n\n4. PERCENTAGES AND PERCENTAGE CHANGE\nPercentage = (part ÷ whole) × 100.\nPercentage change = [(new value − old value) ÷ old value] × 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA commodity's price increases from ₦80 to ₦100.\nChange = ₦20.\nPercentage increase = (20 ÷ 80) × 100 = 25%, NOT 20%.\nThe denominator is the ORIGINAL value.\n\nIf price then falls from ₦100 to ₦80, the fall is (20 ÷ 100) × 100 = 20%. A 25% rise followed by a 20% fall returns to the starting price.\n\n5. AVERAGES — MEAN, MEDIAN AND MODE\nData: 2, 3, 3, 4, 18.\n• Mean = (2 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 18) ÷ 5 = 6.\n• Median = middle observation after arranging values = 3.\n• Mode = most frequent observation = 3.\n• Range = highest − lowest = 18 − 2 = 16.\n\nThe mean is sensitive to extreme values; the median is often more representative for skewed income distributions.\n\nEVEN NUMBER OF OBSERVATIONS: For 2, 4, 6, 10, median = (4 + 6) ÷ 2 = 5.\n\n6. WEIGHTED MEAN\nSome observations count more than others. Weighted mean = sum of (value × weight) ÷ sum of weights.\n\nEXAMPLE\nA student scores 70 on a test worth 40% and 80 on an exam worth 60%.\nWeighted score = (70 × 0.4) + (80 × 0.6) = 76.\nDo not simply average 70 and 80 unless both carry equal weights.\n\n7. INDEX NUMBERS\nAn index compares a value with a base period, usually assigned 100.\nSimple price index = (current price ÷ base-period price) × 100.\n\nEXAMPLE\nBase-year price ₦200; current price ₦250.\nIndex = (250 ÷ 200) × 100 = 125.\nInterpretation: the price is 25% ABOVE the base-period level, not 125% above it.\n\nAn index of 90 means 10% below the base. A consumer price index (CPI) summarises changes in prices of a representative basket, using weights.\n\n8. SIMPLE RELATIONSHIPS AND EQUATIONS\nA linear relationship may be written Qd = a − bP, where a is the intercept and b is the positive coefficient showing how quantity demanded falls as price rises.\n\nEXAMPLE\nQd = 50 − 2P. At P = 10, Qd = 50 − 20 = 30.\nAt P = 15, Qd = 20.\nInterpret the result before using it: quantities should be economically meaningful.\n\n9. CORRELATION IS NOT CAUSATION\nTwo variables may move together without one causing the other. Ice-cream sales and swimming incidents may both rise in hot weather; heat is a relevant third factor.\n\nJAMB EXAM CHECKLIST\n• Read chart labels and units.\n• Identify the base value before calculating a percentage.\n• Arrange data before finding a median.\n• Distinguish a histogram from a bar chart.\n• Interpret an index relative to 100.\n• Do not infer causation from correlation alone.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — WORKING WITH ECONOMIC DATA\n\n10. FREQUENCY DISTRIBUTIONS\nA frequency distribution groups observations and counts their occurrences. For the data 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, the frequency of 4 is three, while the total frequency is seven.\n\nFor grouped observations, the estimated mean is:\nMean ≈ Σ(f × class midpoint) ÷ Σf.\nUse the midpoint of each class interval, multiply by its frequency, add the products and divide by the total frequency.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nScores 0–9: frequency 2, midpoint 4.5.\nScores 10–19: frequency 3, midpoint 14.5.\nEstimated mean = [(2 × 4.5) + (3 × 14.5)] ÷ 5\n= (9 + 43.5) ÷ 5 = 10.5.\nIt is an estimate because the individual observations within each interval are unknown.\n\n11. CUMULATIVE FREQUENCY\nCumulative frequency is the running total of frequencies. If three classes have frequencies 4, 6 and 5, cumulative frequencies are 4, 10 and 15. The final cumulative frequency equals the total number of observations.\n\nAn ogive is a cumulative-frequency curve. It can be used to estimate medians, quartiles and percentiles from grouped data.\n\n12. MEASURES OF DISPERSION\nCentral tendency tells us the typical value; dispersion tells us how spread out the values are.\n• Range = maximum − minimum.\n• Variance = average squared deviation from the mean, with population and sample formulas differing in their denominators.\n• Standard deviation = square root of variance.\n• A larger standard deviation generally indicates greater dispersion when comparing observations measured on the same scale.\n\nEXAMPLE: Two classes may have the same average test score of 60, but one has scores tightly clustered near 60 while the other ranges from 20 to 100. Their means match; their dispersion does not.\n\n13. REAL AND NOMINAL VALUES\nNominal values are measured in current money terms. Real values adjust for price-level changes.\n\nSimplified deflation:\nReal value at base-year prices ≈ (nominal value ÷ price index) × 100, when the index base is 100.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nNominal income = ₦150,000; relevant price index = 125.\nReal income in base-year purchasing-power terms ≈ ₦150,000 ÷ 125 × 100 = ₦120,000.\nA higher nominal income does not necessarily mean higher purchasing power if prices rise faster.\n\n14. SIMPLE WEIGHTED PRICE INDEX\nSuppose a basket contains 2 units of rice and 1 unit of oil.\nBase prices: rice ₦100, oil ₦200 → basket ₦400.\nCurrent prices: rice ₦150, oil ₦250 → same basket ₦550.\nFixed-basket index = (550 ÷ 400) × 100 = 137.5.\nInterpretation: the basket costs 37.5% more than in the base period. This illustrates the principle of a Laspeyres-type fixed-basket index.\n\n15. CAUTION WITH GRAPH SCALES\nA bar chart whose vertical axis starts at 90 rather than zero can exaggerate differences. Read the numerical axis before comparing visual heights. For a line graph, check whether the horizontal axis represents equal time intervals.\n\n16. FIVE COMMON CALCULATION ERRORS\n• Using the NEW value rather than the OLD value as the percentage-change denominator.\n• Forgetting to sort values before finding the median.\n• Confusing frequency with cumulative frequency.\n• Interpreting an index of 140 as a 140% increase rather than 40%.\n• Using a simple average where observations have different weights.\n\nEXAM PRACTICE — SOLVE BEFORE READING\nA commodity's price increases from ₦240 to ₦300. Calculate the percentage increase.\nSolution: (300 − 240)/240 × 100 = 25%.\nA pie chart shows 25% of household spending on rent. Angle?\nSolution: 0.25 × 360° = 90°.\nScores are 4, 7, 8, 9, 22. Which is more resistant to the extreme value 22?\nSolution: median (8), not mean (10).\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Price rises from ₦80 to ₦100. Percentage rise? 25%.\n2. What pie-chart angle represents 15%? 54°.\n3. Find the median of 2, 3, 3, 4, 18. Answer: 3.\n4. Base price ₦100, current price ₦125. Index? 125, meaning 25% higher.\n5. Which average is most affected by an extreme value? Mean.\n6. In a standard demand graph, which axis carries price? Vertical axis."}},{"id":"demand-supply","old":{"title":"Demand, supply and price determination","body":"Demand is the quantity of a good consumers are willing and able to buy at a given price over a period. Desire without ability to pay is not effective demand.\n\nLaw of demand: ceteris paribus, the higher the price, the lower the quantity demanded, and the lower the price, the higher the quantity demanded. The demand curve slopes downward from left to right.\n\nDeterminants of demand (they SHIFT the curve): income, prices of related goods (substitutes and complements), tastes and fashion, population, expectations of future prices, advertising, government policy (taxes, subsidies), season and weather.\n• Change in quantity demanded = movement ALONG the curve, caused only by the good's own price.\n• Change in demand (increase or decrease) = SHIFT of the whole curve.\n\nTypes: joint (complementary) demand, e.g. cars and petrol; competitive demand (substitutes), e.g. Peak and Three Crowns milk; derived demand, e.g. labour or cement derived from demand for houses; composite demand, e.g. palm oil for cooking and soap.\n\nExceptional demand curves (slope upward): Giffen goods, goods of ostentation (Veblen goods, e.g. luxury cars), expectation of further price rises, and ignorance.\n\nSupply is the quantity producers are willing and able to offer at a given price. Law of supply: the higher the price, the higher the quantity supplied; the curve slopes upward. Determinants: cost of production, technology, prices of other goods, weather (for farm produce), number of firms, taxes and subsidies, goals of the firm. Joint supply: beef and hides; competitive supply: land used for maize or cassava.\n\nEquilibrium: where quantity demanded = quantity supplied. Below equilibrium price there is excess demand (shortage) and price rises; above it there is excess supply (surplus) and price falls.\nWorked example: Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P. At equilibrium 100 − 2P = 20 + 2P, so 80 = 4P, P = ₦20; Q = 100 − 40 = 60 units.\n\nShifts: an increase in demand raises both price and quantity; an increase in supply lowers price and raises quantity.\n\nPrice control:\n• Maximum price (price ceiling), set BELOW equilibrium to protect consumers: causes shortage, queues, rationing, hoarding and black markets (e.g. controlled petrol prices in Nigeria before deregulation in 2023).\n• Minimum price (price floor), set ABOVE equilibrium to protect producers or workers: causes surplus, which government may buy and store. The national minimum wage is a price floor in the labour market.\n• Rationing: sharing scarce goods by coupons, quotas or queues.\n\nCommon traps:\n• \"Demand falls\" (shift) is not the same as \"quantity demanded falls\" (movement).\n• A ceiling only has effect below equilibrium, a floor only above it.\n• A rise in the price of a substitute RAISES demand for the other good; a rise in the price of a complement LOWERS it.\n• The law of supply is a DIRECT relationship; the law of demand is INVERSE.\n• Income is a determinant of demand, not of supply."},"new":{"title":"Demand, supply and price determination","body":"DEMAND, SUPPLY AND PRICE DETERMINATION — FOLLOW THE CAUSE\n\nWHY THIS LESSON MATTERS\nJAMB repeatedly tests whether a change is a movement ALONG a curve or a SHIFT of the curve. Begin every question by asking: What changed first?\n\nDEMAND AND SUPPLY — DEFINITIONS, SCHEDULES AND CURVES\n\nDemand refers to the various quantities of a commodity that consumers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a given period, other things remaining constant. It is represented by the whole demand schedule or demand curve.\n\nQUANTITY DEMANDED is ONE specific quantity consumers are willing and able to buy at ONE particular price during that period. It is represented by a single point on the demand curve.\n\nEXAMPLE — RICE DEMAND SCHEDULE\nPrice per bag:         ₦50,000 | ₦60,000 | ₦70,000\nQuantity demanded:        100 |      80 |      60 bags\n\nThe entire schedule is DEMAND. “80 bags at ₦60,000” is a QUANTITY DEMANDED. If price falls from ₦60,000 to ₦50,000 and purchases rise from 80 to 100 bags, there is an INCREASE IN QUANTITY DEMANDED (movement along the same demand curve), not an increase in demand.\n\nSupply refers to the various quantities of a commodity that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at different prices during a given period, other things remaining constant.\n\nQUANTITY SUPPLIED is ONE specific quantity producers or sellers are willing and able to offer for sale at ONE particular price. It is a single point on the supply curve.\n\nEXAMPLE — TOMATO SUPPLY SCHEDULE\nPrice per basket:      ₦5,000 | ₦7,000 | ₦9,000\nQuantity supplied:          20 |      35 |      50 baskets\n\nThe whole schedule is SUPPLY. “35 baskets at ₦7,000” is QUANTITY SUPPLIED. If the price rises to ₦9,000 and sellers offer 50 baskets, quantity supplied increases along the SAME supply curve.\n\nJAMB EXAM RULE:\n• Demand/supply = the whole price–quantity relationship (curve or schedule).\n• Quantity demanded/supplied = the amount at one specified price (a point).\n• Own-price change = movement along the curve.\n• Non-price determinant change = shift of the curve.\n• Consumers DEMAND; producers/sellers SUPPLY. The word “supply” does not mean the quantity a consumer wants to buy.\n\n1. DEMAND: WILLING AND ABLE TO BUY\nThe law of demand describes the inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded, other things equal. Wanting a phone without purchasing power is not effective demand.\n\nLaw of demand: other things equal (ceteris paribus), a higher price leads to lower quantity demanded, and a lower price to higher quantity demanded.\n\nEXAMPLE — OWN-PRICE MOVEMENT\nIf a bag of rice becomes cheaper and consumers buy more rice, that is an INCREASE IN QUANTITY DEMANDED: movement down along the existing demand curve.\n\n2. WHAT SHIFTS DEMAND?\nA change in something OTHER than the good's own price can shift the whole demand curve.\n• Income: demand for a normal good generally rises as income rises; demand for an inferior good may fall.\n• Tastes, fashion and advertising.\n• Prices of substitutes: if tea becomes dearer, demand for coffee may increase.\n• Prices of complements: if printers become dearer, demand for ink cartridges may decrease.\n• Population or number of buyers.\n• Expectations of future prices or income.\n• Seasonal conditions.\n\nA RIGHTWARD demand shift means more is demanded at every given price. A LEFTWARD shift means less.\n\nJAMB TRAP: “Increase in demand” means a shift right. “Increase in quantity demanded” means movement along the same curve.\n\n3. SUPPLY: WILLING AND ABLE TO SELL\nThe law of supply describes the direct relationship between price and quantity supplied, other things equal.\n\nLaw of supply: other things equal, a higher price generally encourages a larger quantity supplied. The usual supply curve slopes upward.\n\nEXAMPLE — OWN-PRICE MOVEMENT\nIf the price of tomatoes rises and farmers bring more tomatoes to market, quantity supplied increases along the supply curve.\n\n4. WHAT SHIFTS SUPPLY?\n• Production costs: higher fuel or fertiliser costs tend to shift supply LEFT.\n• Technology: better equipment can shift supply RIGHT.\n• Taxes on production generally shift supply LEFT; subsidies may shift it RIGHT.\n• Weather and natural conditions affect farm output.\n• Number of producers and availability of inputs.\n• Expectations and prices of alternative products.\n\nA shift right means producers can offer more at each price; a shift left means less.\n\n5. MARKET EQUILIBRIUM — WHERE PLANS MATCH\nEquilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nSuppose Qd = 100 − 2P and Qs = 20 + 2P.\nSet Qd = Qs:\n100 − 2P = 20 + 2P\n80 = 4P\nEquilibrium price P = 20.\nEquilibrium quantity Q = 100 − 2(20) = 60.\nCheck supply: 20 + 2(20) = 60.\n\nAt a price ABOVE equilibrium, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded: SURPLUS. Sellers face pressure to lower prices.\nAt a price BELOW equilibrium, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied: SHORTAGE. Buyers compete for limited stock, creating upward price pressure.\n\n6. PREDICTING A NEW EQUILIBRIUM\n• Demand rises, supply unchanged → equilibrium PRICE rises and QUANTITY rises.\n• Demand falls, supply unchanged → price falls and quantity falls.\n• Supply rises, demand unchanged → price falls and quantity rises.\n• Supply falls, demand unchanged → price rises and quantity falls.\n\nIf demand and supply both shift, the effect on price or quantity may be uncertain without knowing their relative sizes.\n\nEXAMPLE: Better harvests increase tomato supply. Other things equal, market price falls and quantity sold rises.\n\n7. PRICE CEILINGS AND FLOORS\nA price CEILING is a legal maximum. If set BELOW equilibrium and enforced, it tends to create a shortage. Example: a rent cap below the market-clearing rent may generate excess demand for available housing.\n\nA price FLOOR is a legal minimum. If set ABOVE equilibrium and enforced, it tends to create a surplus. Example: a minimum guaranteed agricultural price may encourage output beyond purchases.\n\nA ceiling above equilibrium or floor below equilibrium is non-binding and need not change the market outcome.\n\n8. RELATED GOODS\n• Substitutes: consumers can use one instead of the other (tea and coffee).\n• Complements: goods used together (cars and petrol).\n• Normal goods: demand usually rises with income.\n• Inferior goods: demand usually falls as income rises, all else equal.\n\nEXAM TRAP: “Inferior” describes the income–demand relationship; it does not necessarily mean low quality.\n\n9. EXCEPTIONS AND LIMITATIONS\nThe law of demand assumes other factors remain constant. Rare Giffen-good behaviour and some status-driven Veblen-good purchases are discussed as exceptions or special cases, but do not assume every expensive luxury violates the law of demand.\n\nQUICK DIAGNOSTIC\nAsk three questions:\n1. Did the good's OWN price change? Think movement along the curve.\n2. Did another factor change? Think curve shift.\n3. Which curve shifted and in which direction? Then predict equilibrium.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — MULTIPLE SHIFTS AND MARKET POLICY\n\n10. DERIVING EQUILIBRIUM FROM A TABLE\nSuppose a market has the following schedules:\nPrice ₦10: quantity demanded 90, quantity supplied 30.\nPrice ₦20: quantity demanded 70, quantity supplied 50.\nPrice ₦30: quantity demanded 50, quantity supplied 50.\nPrice ₦40: quantity demanded 30, quantity supplied 70.\n\nAt ₦30, buyers and sellers each plan to trade 50 units. This is equilibrium.\nAt ₦20, excess demand = 70 − 50 = 20 units (shortage).\nAt ₦40, excess supply = 70 − 30 = 40 units (surplus).\n\n11. SIMULTANEOUS CHANGES — WHAT CAN WE KNOW?\nIf demand AND supply both rise:\n• Equilibrium quantity definitely rises under standard upward supply/downward demand curves.\n• Equilibrium price may rise, fall or remain unchanged depending on the relative shifts.\n\nIf demand rises while supply falls:\n• Equilibrium price definitely rises.\n• Equilibrium quantity is uncertain.\n\nIf demand falls while supply rises:\n• Equilibrium price definitely falls.\n• Equilibrium quantity is uncertain.\n\nIf both demand and supply fall:\n• Equilibrium quantity definitely falls.\n• Equilibrium price is uncertain.\n\nThis is a favourite reasoning trap: NEVER claim both price and quantity outcomes are certain when opposing shifts make one ambiguous.\n\n12. DIRECT, DERIVED, JOINT AND COMPETITIVE DEMAND\n• Direct demand: a consumer wants a good for its own use, such as bread.\n• Derived demand: demand for an input depends on demand for the final product, such as labour to build houses.\n• Joint demand: complementary goods are demanded together, such as cars and fuel.\n• Competitive demand: substitutes compete for buyers, such as tea and coffee.\n\nJOINT SUPPLY occurs when one productive process yields multiple outputs, such as beef and hides from cattle. Do not confuse joint demand (goods used together) with joint supply (products produced together).\n\n13. MARKET STRUCTURE AND PRICE SETTING\nIn a highly competitive market, individual sellers have limited ability to set prices independently. In a monopoly, one seller may have greater pricing power, although buyers' willingness to pay and substitutes still constrain demand.\n\n“Price taker” describes a firm that accepts the market price; “price maker” describes a firm with some control over its selling price. Do not assume every large firm is a monopoly.\n\n14. TAXES, SUBSIDIES AND INCIDENCE\nA per-unit tax on sellers raises the cost of supplying each unit and shifts supply upward/left in the standard diagram. Buyers typically pay a higher price, sellers receive a lower net-of-tax price, and equilibrium quantity falls.\n\nWHO BEARS THE TAX?\nThe less price-responsive side of the market generally bears more of the tax burden. If demand is very inelastic relative to supply, consumers tend to bear more; if supply is very inelastic relative to demand, producers tend to bear more.\n\nA production subsidy generally shifts supply right, tending to lower the consumer price and increase quantity, other things equal.\n\n15. GOVERNMENT PRICE CONTROLS — CONSEQUENCES\nA binding ceiling can create queues, rationing or unofficial markets because the quantity demanded exceeds the quantity offered. A binding floor can create unsold stocks or require public purchases if government guarantees the minimum price.\n\nPolicy goals matter: a rent ceiling may seek affordability, but its effects depend on housing supply, enforcement and complementary policies. The simple model predicts pressures, not every real-world detail.\n\n16. APPLY THE LOGIC TO NIGERIA\n• Higher fertiliser prices: supply of farm produce may shift left.\n• Better roads reducing transport losses: supply available in urban markets may shift right.\n• Rising incomes: demand for normal goods may shift right.\n• An increase in the price of a close substitute: demand for the other product may shift right.\n\nTEST YOURSELF — REASON, DON'T GUESS\nA flood destroys part of a tomato harvest while household demand stays unchanged. What happens?\nAnswer: supply shifts left; equilibrium price rises and quantity falls.\nDemand and supply both increase. What must rise?\nAnswer: equilibrium quantity; the price effect is uncertain.\nA tax raises the price paid by consumers by ₦30 but sellers' net receipt falls ₦20. What is the per-unit tax?\nAnswer: ₦50, the wedge between the two prices.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Rice becomes cheaper and consumers buy more. Demand shift or movement? Movement along demand: higher quantity demanded.\n2. Tea becomes dearer. What may happen to coffee demand? It shifts right if coffee is a substitute.\n3. A binding maximum price below equilibrium causes what? Shortage.\n4. A binding minimum price above equilibrium causes what? Surplus.\n5. Better farming technology does what to supply? Shifts supply right.\n6. If demand increases while supply stays fixed, what happens? Equilibrium price and quantity rise."}},{"id":"elasticity","old":{"title":"Elasticity of demand and supply","body":"Elasticity measures how responsive quantity is to a change in one of its determinants.\n\n1. Price elasticity of demand (PED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price (ignore the minus sign).\n% change = (change ÷ original value) × 100.\nWorked example: price of a loaf rises from ₦1,000 to ₦1,200; quantity demanded falls from 50 to 35.\nStep 1: % change in Qd = (15 ÷ 50) × 100 = 30%.\nStep 2: % change in P = (200 ÷ 1,000) × 100 = 20%.\nStep 3: PED = 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5 (elastic).\nTypes: PED > 1 elastic; PED < 1 inelastic; PED = 1 unitary; PED = 0 perfectly inelastic (vertical curve); PED = ∞ perfectly elastic (horizontal curve).\nTotal revenue test: if demand is elastic, a price rise reduces total revenue; if inelastic, a price rise increases it; if unitary, total revenue is unchanged. In the example, TR before = ₦1,000 × 50 = ₦50,000 and after = ₦1,200 × 35 = ₦42,000: TR falls, as expected for elastic demand.\nDeterminants of PED: availability of substitutes, whether a necessity or luxury, proportion of income spent, number of uses, habit (cigarettes), time period, durability.\n\n2. Income elasticity of demand (YED) = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income. Positive for normal goods (above 1 for luxuries), negative for inferior goods.\n\n3. Cross elasticity of demand (XED) = % change in quantity demanded of good X ÷ % change in price of good Y. Positive for substitutes, negative for complements, zero for unrelated goods.\n\n4. Price elasticity of supply (PES) = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price. Perfectly inelastic supply is vertical (land, a stadium's seats); perfectly elastic supply is horizontal. Supply is more elastic with time, spare capacity, storable goods and mobile factors. Farm produce supply is inelastic in the short run because crops take time to grow.\n\nIncidence of tax: who finally bears an indirect tax.\n• If demand is inelastic (or supply elastic), consumers bear most of the tax.\n• If demand is elastic (or supply inelastic), producers bear most.\n• Perfectly inelastic demand: consumers bear all; perfectly elastic demand: producers bear all.\nExample: a ₦100 tax per crate raises the market price from ₦2,000 to ₦2,070. Consumers bear ₦70 and producers ₦30.\n\nUses of elasticity: government chooses goods with inelastic demand to tax for revenue; firms use it for pricing and price discrimination; it explains the effects of devaluation.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Always divide by the ORIGINAL value when computing % change.\n• PED is quantity over price, not price over quantity.\n• A vertical demand or supply curve is perfectly INELASTIC.\n• Negative XED means complements; negative YED means inferior goods.\n• Inelastic demand plus a price rise means total revenue rises."},"new":{"title":"Elasticity of demand and supply","body":"ELASTICITY — MEASURE HOW STRONGLY BUYERS AND SELLERS RESPOND\n\n1. WHAT ELASTICITY MEANS\nElasticity is RESPONSIVENESS, not simply a change in quantity. It measures the percentage change in one variable caused by a percentage change in another.\n\n2. PRICE ELASTICITY OF DEMAND (PED)\nPED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price.\nDemand normally moves in the opposite direction to price, so PED is often negative. In JAMB classifications, use its ABSOLUTE value unless the question asks for the signed coefficient.\n\n• Elastic: |PED| > 1 — quantity changes proportionately more than price.\n• Inelastic: |PED| < 1 — quantity changes proportionately less.\n• Unit elastic: |PED| = 1.\n• Perfectly inelastic: PED = 0, a vertical demand curve.\n• Perfectly elastic: an idealised horizontal demand curve, PED tending to infinity.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE — ORIGINAL-BASE METHOD\nPrice rises from ₦1,000 to ₦1,200; quantity falls from 50 to 35.\n% change in price = 200/1,000 × 100 = 20%.\n% change in quantity = −15/50 × 100 = −30%.\nPED = −30%/20% = −1.5. Absolute value 1.5: elastic.\n\nMIDPOINT METHOD: Some questions specify arc or midpoint elasticity. Then divide each change by the AVERAGE of old and new values, not the original. Always follow the question's stated method.\n\n3. ELASTICITY AND TOTAL REVENUE\nTotal revenue (TR) = price × quantity sold.\n• Elastic demand: a price CUT generally raises TR; a price rise lowers TR.\n• Inelastic demand: a price RISE generally raises TR; a price cut lowers TR.\n• Unit elasticity: small price changes leave TR approximately unchanged.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nInitially 50 units sell at ₦1,000: TR = ₦50,000.\nAfter the price rises to ₦1,200, 35 sell: TR = ₦42,000.\nRevenue falls, consistent with elastic demand over this change.\n\n4. WHAT DETERMINES PED?\n• Availability of close substitutes: more substitutes usually means more elastic demand.\n• Necessity versus luxury: necessities tend to be less elastic.\n• Share of consumer income: expensive items may be more elastic.\n• Time to adjust: demand often becomes more elastic over longer periods.\n• How narrowly a product is defined: one brand usually has more substitutes than an entire product category.\n\nEXAM TRAP: Inelastic does NOT mean demand never changes; it means the percentage response is smaller than the percentage price change.\n\n5. INCOME ELASTICITY OF DEMAND (YED)\nYED = % change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in income.\n• Positive: normal good.\n• Negative: inferior good.\n• Greater than 1: income-elastic demand, often associated with luxuries.\n• Between 0 and 1: income-inelastic normal demand, often associated with necessities.\n\nEXAMPLE: Income rises 10%; demand for a service rises 20%. YED = +2. It is a normal, income-elastic good.\n\n6. CROSS-PRICE ELASTICITY (XED)\nXED = % change in demand for good A ÷ % change in price of good B.\n• Positive: substitutes, such as tea and coffee.\n• Negative: complements, such as printers and ink.\n• Near zero: weakly related goods.\n\nEXAMPLE: Tea price rises 10%; coffee demand rises 5%. XED = +0.5: substitutes.\n\n7. PRICE ELASTICITY OF SUPPLY (PES)\nPES = % change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price.\nSupply is usually positively related to price.\n• Elastic supply: PES > 1.\n• Inelastic supply: PES < 1.\n• Perfectly inelastic supply: PES = 0 (vertical curve).\n• Perfectly elastic supply: idealised horizontal curve.\n\nSupply tends to be more elastic when firms have spare capacity, available inputs, stocks and enough time to expand production. Fresh farm produce may be relatively inelastic in the short run.\n\n8. GRAPH AND EXAM WARNINGS\nA STEEP curve often suggests relative inelasticity and a FLAT curve relative elasticity, but slope and elasticity are not identical concepts: elasticity also depends on the price–quantity point and measurement units. On a straight-line demand curve, PED varies along the line.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — ELASTICITY IN MARKETS AND EXAMS\n\n9. POINT ELASTICITY VS ARC ELASTICITY\nThe original-base percentage method measures responsiveness using a chosen starting point. Reversing the direction of a price change can produce a different numerical percentage.\n\nThe midpoint (arc) method avoids this direction problem for two observations:\nArc PED = [(Q2 − Q1)/((Q1 + Q2)/2)] ÷ [(P2 − P1)/((P1 + P2)/2)].\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nPrice rises from ₦10 to ₦12; quantity falls from 100 to 80.\nQuantity percentage change using midpoint = −20/90 = −22.22%.\nPrice percentage change using midpoint = 2/11 = 18.18%.\nArc PED ≈ −1.22; magnitude 1.22, elastic.\n\nCompare with original-base method: −20%/20% = −1, unit elastic. The answers differ because the METHODS differ. Use the one the exam specifies.\n\n10. ELASTICITY ALONG A STRAIGHT-LINE DEMAND CURVE\nFor a downward-sloping straight-line demand curve, elasticity is not constant along the curve:\n• Upper portion (high price, low quantity): generally elastic.\n• Midpoint: unit elastic.\n• Lower portion (low price, high quantity): generally inelastic.\n\nDo not confuse a constant SLOPE with constant ELASTICITY. Elasticity incorporates proportional changes and therefore depends on the point.\n\n11. DEMAND ELASTICITY AND REVENUE — WHY IT MATTERS\nSuppose a cinema sells 100 tickets at ₦2,000 each: revenue ₦200,000.\nIt lowers the price to ₦1,800 and sells 130 tickets: revenue ₦234,000.\nThe price reduction increased revenue because the quantity response was strong enough.\n\nBut revenue is not profit. If serving more customers raises costs substantially, greater revenue need not mean greater profit. JAMB questions about “total expenditure” or “total revenue” are asking about price × quantity, not necessarily profit.\n\n12. SUPPLY ELASTICITY AND TIME\nA farmer cannot immediately grow more yams when prices rise today. Over another planting season, land and inputs can be reallocated, making supply more responsive.\n\nFor a manufactured product, spare factory capacity may allow a quick output increase. If all machines are fully utilised and skilled workers are scarce, supply may be relatively inelastic even when prices rise.\n\n13. ELASTICITY AND TAX POLICY\nIf demand is highly inelastic, a tax-induced price increase may reduce purchases only modestly, although consumers may face a greater burden. If demand is elastic, the same tax may cause a larger contraction in quantity.\n\nCAUTION: “Government should tax inelastic goods” is a policy judgement, not a universal recommendation. Health effects, fairness and distributional impacts also matter.\n\n14. PRICE ELASTICITY OF DEMAND VS SLOPE\nSlope in a standard price-on-vertical-axis demand graph is change in price divided by change in quantity. PED compares PERCENTAGE changes in quantity and price. A graph's apparent steepness depends on its axes and scales; it is not enough to calculate elasticity numerically.\n\n15. EXAM DECISION TREE\n• Question changes the good's own price and asks about its quantity demanded → PED.\n• Question changes consumer income → YED.\n• Question changes the price of a DIFFERENT good → XED.\n• Question changes the price received by producers and asks about quantity supplied → PES.\n• Question asks whether revenue rises after a price cut → consider PED magnitude.\n\nFURTHER WORKED CHECKS\nA good's demand rises 12% when income rises 8%. YED = 12/8 = +1.5: normal and income-elastic.\nDemand for A falls 6% when price of B rises 10%. XED = −0.6: complements.\nSupply rises 5% after price rises 20%. PES = 0.25: inelastic.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Price rises 10%; quantity demanded falls 30%. PED magnitude? 3, elastic.\n2. What happens to revenue when price rises under elastic demand? It generally falls.\n3. Income rises 20%; demand falls 10%. YED? −0.5, inferior good.\n4. Cross-price elasticity is positive. Relationship? Substitutes.\n5. Why is housing supply often inelastic in the short run? Construction takes time.\n6. PED equals zero. What is the curve? Vertical; perfectly inelastic demand."}},{"id":"consumer-behaviour","old":{"title":"Theory of consumer behaviour","body":"Utility is the satisfaction a consumer gets from consuming a good or service. It is subjective and, in cardinal theory, measured in imaginary units called utils.\n\n• Total utility (TU): the total satisfaction from all units consumed.\n• Marginal utility (MU): the extra satisfaction from one more unit: MU = change in TU ÷ change in quantity.\n• Average utility = TU ÷ quantity.\n\nLaw of diminishing marginal utility: as a consumer takes more and more units of a good in a period, other things being equal, the marginal utility of each additional unit eventually falls.\nWorked example (sachets of water):\nUnits: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nTU: 10, 18, 24, 27, 27\nMU: 10, 8, 6, 3, 0\nTU is maximum (27) when MU = 0; if a 6th sachet gave TU of 25, MU would be −2 (disutility).\nAssumptions: units are consumed continuously, are of the same size and quality, and tastes and income are unchanged.\n\nConsumer equilibrium (one good): the consumer buys until MU = price (in money terms).\nEqui-marginal principle (many goods): the consumer is in equilibrium when\nMUa ÷ Pa = MUb ÷ Pb = … = MUn ÷ Pn, with all income spent.\nExample: if MU of rice = 40 at ₦20 per unit and MU of beans = 30 at ₦10, then MU/P is 2 for rice and 3 for beans, so the consumer should buy more beans (lowering its MU) and less rice until the ratios are equal.\n\nThe law of diminishing MU explains why the demand curve slopes downward: a consumer will pay less for extra units that give less satisfaction.\n\nParadox of value (water and diamonds): water has high total utility but low marginal utility because it is plentiful; diamonds are scarce so their marginal utility and price are high.\n\nConsumer surplus: the difference between what a consumer is willing to pay and what he actually pays. If Ngozi would pay ₦5,000 for a shirt but buys it for ₦3,500, her consumer surplus is ₦1,500. On a graph it is the area below the demand curve and above the price line.\n\nIndifference curve approach (ordinal utility): an indifference curve shows combinations of two goods that give the same satisfaction. Indifference curves slope downward, are convex to the origin (diminishing marginal rate of substitution), never intersect, and higher curves mean more satisfaction. The budget line shows what the consumer can afford. Equilibrium is where the budget line is tangent to the highest attainable indifference curve.\n\nCommon traps:\n• It is MARGINAL utility that diminishes; total utility still rises while MU is positive.\n• TU is at its maximum when MU is zero, not when MU is highest.\n• Consumer surplus = willingness to pay − actual price, not price − cost.\n• Indifference curves cannot cross.\n• Cardinal = measurable utils (marginal utility theory); ordinal = ranking only (indifference curves)."},"new":{"title":"Theory of consumer behaviour","body":"CONSUMER BEHAVIOUR — WHY PEOPLE CHOOSE ONE BASKET OVER ANOTHER\n\n1. UTILITY AND CHOICE\nUtility means satisfaction or benefit derived from consuming goods and services. It is subjective: the same product can be valued differently by different consumers.\n\nCardinal utility theory uses imaginary units called “utils”. Ordinal utility theory ranks bundles without needing to measure satisfaction numerically.\n\n2. TOTAL, MARGINAL AND AVERAGE UTILITY\n• Total utility (TU): total satisfaction from all units consumed.\n• Marginal utility (MU): EXTRA satisfaction from one additional unit.\n• Average utility (AU): TU divided by quantity.\n\nMU = change in TU ÷ change in quantity.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nUnits of oranges: 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nTotal utility:      0 | 12 | 22 | 30 | 35 | 35\nMarginal utility:   – | 12 | 10 |  8 |  5 |  0\n\nThe fourth orange adds 5 utils; the fifth adds none. Total utility reaches its maximum where MU is zero, in this example. If MU becomes negative, consuming another unit reduces TU.\n\n3. LAW OF DIMINISHING MARGINAL UTILITY\nAs a consumer consumes successive units of a good within a given period, holding other relevant conditions constant, the ADDITIONAL satisfaction tends to fall.\n\nEXAMPLE: The first glass of water when thirsty may be extremely satisfying. The second helps less; a fifth may provide almost no additional satisfaction.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Diminishing MU does not mean TU must immediately fall. TU can keep rising while MU remains positive but gets smaller.\n\n4. CONSUMER EQUILIBRIUM — CARDINAL APPROACH\nA consumer with a fixed budget seeks maximum total utility.\nFor two divisible goods X and Y, an interior optimum under the standard assumptions satisfies:\nMUx/Px = MUy/Py,\nwhile the budget is exhausted when more spending can increase utility.\n\nInterpretation: the marginal utility obtained per naira spent is equal across goods. If one good yields more marginal utility per naira, the consumer can improve satisfaction by reallocating spending toward it, subject to constraints.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nMU of an extra snack = 20 utils; snack price = ₦200 → 0.10 utils per naira.\nMU of an extra drink = 15 utils; drink price = ₦100 → 0.15 utils per naira.\nAt these margins, the drink yields greater utility per naira; reallocating toward drinks may increase total satisfaction.\n\n5. INDIFFERENCE CURVES — ORDINAL APPROACH\nAn indifference curve joins combinations of two goods that give the consumer the SAME level of satisfaction.\n\nStandard properties under usual preferences:\n• Downward sloping: more of one good compensates for less of the other.\n• Higher curves represent greater satisfaction.\n• Curves do not intersect.\n• Typically convex to the origin because of diminishing marginal rate of substitution (MRS).\n\nMRS describes how much of Y the consumer is willing to give up for an additional unit of X while keeping utility constant.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Different points on the SAME indifference curve provide equal satisfaction, even though their quantities differ.\n\n6. THE BUDGET LINE\nA budget line shows all combinations of two goods that use a consumer's available income at given prices.\n\nEquation: PxX + PyY = M, where M is income.\nIntercepts:\n• Maximum X if only X is bought = M/Px.\n• Maximum Y if only Y is bought = M/Py.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nIncome = ₦1,200; X costs ₦200 and Y costs ₦300.\nMaximum X = 1,200/200 = 6.\nMaximum Y = 1,200/300 = 4.\nA basket of 3X and 2Y costs 3(200) + 2(300) = ₦1,200: exactly affordable.\n\nA basket BELOW the budget line costs less than the available income. A basket ABOVE it is unaffordable.\n\n7. ORDINAL CONSUMER EQUILIBRIUM\nUnder standard smooth, convex preferences, an interior optimum occurs where the highest attainable indifference curve is tangent to the budget line:\nMRSxy = Px/Py.\nAt that point the consumer's willingness to trade one good for the other matches the market trade-off.\n\nNot every optimum is a tangency: a corner solution is possible when the consumer buys none of one good.\n\n8. INCOME AND SUBSTITUTION EFFECTS\nWhen the price of X falls:\n• Substitution effect: X becomes cheaper relative to Y, encouraging substitution toward X.\n• Income effect: purchasing power rises because the same income can now buy more.\n\nFor a normal good, both effects increase demand for X when its price falls. For an inferior good, the income effect works in the opposite direction; in a rare Giffen case it can outweigh the substitution effect.\n\n9. CONSUMER SURPLUS\nConsumer surplus is the difference between what a buyer is WILLING to pay and what they ACTUALLY pay.\n\nEXAMPLE: A buyer would pay ₦5,000 for a book but buys it for ₦3,500. Consumer surplus = ₦1,500.\n\nCOMMON EXAM MIX-UPS\nTU = accumulated satisfaction; MU = additional satisfaction.\nDiminishing MU ≠ falling TU while MU is still positive.\nBudget line = affordability; indifference curve = preferences.\nIncome effect ≠ substitution effect.\nConsumer surplus ≠ producer profit.\n\nDEEPER UNDERSTANDING — CONSUMER CHOICE STEP BY STEP\n\n10. A FULL MARGINAL-UTILITY TABLE\nConsider consumption of cups of juice:\nQuantity:          0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6\nTotal utility:     0 | 18| 32| 42| 48| 50| 49\nMarginal utility:  – | 18| 14| 10|  6|  2| −1\n\nNotice the progression:\n• TU rises while MU is positive.\n• TU reaches its maximum at quantity 5 in this discrete example, because adding the sixth unit reduces utility.\n• MU turns negative for the sixth unit.\n• Diminishing MU starts before TU stops rising.\n\nIMPORTANT PRECISION: In a smooth continuous model, TU reaches a maximum where MU = 0. In a discrete table, the highest TU can occur even if the last positive MU is above zero and the next MU is negative.\n\n11. TWO GOODS AND A LIMITED BUDGET\nA student has ₦600 to spend on snacks (₦200 each) and drinks (₦100 each). They cannot simply choose the highest total utility from an unaffordable bundle.\n\nSuppose the marginal utilities of successive snacks are 30, 20, 10 and of successive drinks are 18, 14, 10, 6.\nThe marginal utility per naira of the first snack is 30/200 = 0.15.\nThe first drink gives 18/100 = 0.18.\nThe second drink gives 14/100 = 0.14.\nThe second snack gives 20/200 = 0.10.\n\nA rational allocation compares the NEXT available marginal utility per naira at each step, subject to the whole budget and indivisibility of units. The familiar equality MUx/Px = MUy/Py is a continuous, interior condition, not a rule that every discrete purchase must satisfy exactly.\n\n12. BUDGET-LINE SHIFTS AND ROTATIONS\nIf income rises while both prices remain fixed, the budget line shifts outward in PARALLEL; its slope does not change.\nIf the price of X falls while income and the price of Y stay fixed, the X-intercept moves outward while the Y-intercept remains fixed: the line ROTATES.\nIf both prices and income double, the set of affordable bundles is unchanged.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nIncome ₦1,200; X ₦200; Y ₦300.\nIntercepts: X = 6, Y = 4.\nIncome rises to ₦1,800, prices unchanged.\nNew intercepts: X = 9, Y = 6. This is a parallel outward shift.\n\n13. INDIFFERENCE CURVES — WHY THEY DO NOT CROSS\nSuppose two curves intersect at the same bundle A. Each curve would assign a different satisfaction ranking to other bundles while treating both as indifferent to A. That conflicts with consistent (transitive) preferences. Under standard assumptions, indifference curves therefore cannot intersect.\n\nConvexity reflects a preference for balanced combinations: as a person gets more X and less Y, they are usually willing to sacrifice progressively less Y for an additional X.\n\n14. NORMAL, INFERIOR AND GIFFEN GOODS\nFor a normal good, higher income increases demand. For an inferior good, higher income decreases demand, other things equal.\nA Giffen good is a rare special case of an inferior good where a price rise increases quantity demanded because the negative income effect is unusually strong and outweighs the substitution effect.\n\nNot all inferior goods are Giffen goods. A cheap staple can be inferior without displaying Giffen behaviour.\n\n15. THE LAW OF EQUI-MARGINAL UTILITY\nThe consumer allocates spending across uses so that the last unit of money spent on each gives equal marginal utility, under standard interior conditions. This is also called the principle of equi-marginal returns in consumer choice.\n\nIt does NOT say the total utility of all goods must be equal, or that the consumer must buy equal quantities of each good.\n\n16. EXAM DIAGNOSTIC QUESTIONS\n• “Additional satisfaction from one extra unit” → marginal utility.\n• “All combinations giving equal satisfaction” → indifference curve.\n• “Maximum affordable combinations” → budget line.\n• “The price of one good changes” → budget-line rotation.\n• “Income changes, prices constant” → parallel budget-line shift.\n• “The consumer is willing to pay more than market price” → consumer surplus.\n\nEXTRA REVISION WITH EXPLANATIONS\n1. TU increases from 42 to 48 when quantity increases from 3 to 4. MU = 6.\n2. A consumer's income doubles, and both prices double. Does the budget line change? No; purchasing opportunities are unchanged.\n3. Why can TU rise while MU falls? Each additional unit still adds positive satisfaction, but less than the previous unit.\n4. Is every inferior good a Giffen good? No. Giffen behaviour requires the income effect to outweigh the substitution effect.\n5. At an interior ordinal optimum, what equals the price ratio? Marginal rate of substitution.\n\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. TU rises from 22 to 30 as quantity rises from 2 to 3. MU? 8 utils.\n2. What happens to TU while MU is positive? TU increases.\n3. What does one indifference curve represent? Bundles giving equal satisfaction.\n4. Income ₦1,200, X price ₦200. Maximum X if no Y is bought? 6.\n5. What condition describes an interior cardinal optimum? MUx/Px = MUy/Py.\n6. Willingness to pay ₦5,000, actual price ₦3,500. Consumer surplus? ₦1,500."}},{"id":"production","old":{"title":"Theory of production","body":"Production is the creation of goods and services to satisfy human wants; it is complete only when the good reaches the final consumer. Types: primary (extraction: farming, fishing, mining, crude oil), secondary (manufacturing and construction: cement, refining) and tertiary (services: trade, banking, transport, and direct services such as teaching and medicine).\n\nFactors of production and their rewards:\n• Land (all natural resources) – rent. Fixed in supply, immobile geographically.\n• Labour (human effort, mental and physical) – wages.\n• Capital (man-made goods used to produce other goods, e.g. machines, buildings) – interest.\n• Entrepreneur (organises the others and bears risk) – profit.\n\nDivision of labour and specialisation: breaking production into stages, each done by different workers. Advantages: higher output, skill and speed, use of machines, time saving. Disadvantages: monotony, loss of craftsmanship, interdependence (a strike at one stage stops all), unemployment if demand changes. It is limited by the size of the market, the nature of the product and the availability of capital.\n\nProductivity is output per unit of input (e.g. output per worker). It rises with training, better tools and good management.\n\nShort-run laws of returns (variable proportions): with one factor fixed, adding units of a variable factor first gives increasing returns, then diminishing returns, and finally negative returns.\n• Total product (TP); marginal product MP = change in TP ÷ change in labour; average product AP = TP ÷ labour.\nWorked example (workers on a fixed farm):\nLabour: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\nTP (bags): 10, 24, 33, 40, 40\nMP: 10, 14, 9, 7, 0\nAP: 10, 12, 11, 10, 8\nDiminishing returns set in after the 2nd worker (MP falls from 14 to 9). TP is at its maximum when MP = 0. MP cuts AP at AP's maximum (12).\n\nLong-run returns to scale (all factors varied): increasing returns (output rises by a larger proportion than inputs), constant returns, decreasing returns.\n\nScale of production and economies of scale:\n• Internal economies (within the firm): technical, managerial, financial, marketing (bulk buying and selling), risk-bearing, research, welfare.\n• External economies (from growth of the industry in an area): skilled labour pool, ancillary firms, better roads and power, shared research.\n• Diseconomies of scale: coordination problems, red tape, poor labour relations, and for the industry congestion and higher factor prices.\nSmall firms survive because of a small market, personal services, perishable goods, ease of entry and the desire for independence (e.g. tailors and hairdressers).\n\nLocation of industry: firms locate near raw materials (cement near limestone at Ewekoro and Obajana), markets (Lagos), power, labour, transport, or because of government policy. Localisation is the concentration of an industry in one area (leather in Kano, textiles in Kaduna, shoes and garments in Aba). Advantages: external economies; disadvantages: structural unemployment if the industry declines.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Diminishing returns is a SHORT-RUN law (one factor fixed); returns to scale are LONG-RUN.\n• Diminishing returns begin where MP starts falling, not where TP falls.\n• Money is not capital as a factor; capital means real man-made assets.\n• Rent is the reward for land; interest for capital.\n• Localisation (an industry clustering) differs from location (where a single firm sits)."},"new":{"title":"Theory of production","body":"THEORY OF PRODUCTION — FROM INPUTS TO OUTPUT\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nA firm must decide which resources to use, how many workers to employ, where to locate and whether to expand. JAMB tests the meaning of production, factor rewards, product calculations and the distinction between short-run diminishing returns and long-run returns to scale.\n\n1. WHAT IS PRODUCTION?\nProduction is the creation or enhancement of utility by making goods and services available to satisfy wants. It includes producing a physical object AND providing a service. A teacher, nurse and transporter are economically productive even though they may not manufacture goods.\n\nFour familiar forms of utility are:\n• Form: turning cassava into garri.\n• Place: moving garri from a village to an urban market.\n• Time: storing harvested grain until consumers need it.\n• Possession: enabling ownership or access through exchange.\n\nStages: primary (extracting natural resources: farming, fishing, mining), secondary (manufacturing and construction) and tertiary (services: transport, education, banking and retail). Some classifications separately identify knowledge-intensive or public services; the three-sector division is the standard introductory model.\n\n2. FACTORS OF PRODUCTION\n• Land: natural resources including land, rivers and minerals; conventional reward RENT.\n• Labour: human physical and mental effort; reward WAGES.\n• Capital: produced resources used in further production, such as machinery and tools; conventional reward INTEREST.\n• Entrepreneurship: organisation, innovation and risk-bearing; reward PROFIT.\n\nLand is geographically immobile; its total physical supply is relatively fixed, but the amount available for a particular use can change. Labour is mobile to varying degrees, though skills, family and migration restrictions limit mobility. Machines are capital; money used to buy machines is financial capital.\n\n3. DIVISION OF LABOUR AND SPECIALISATION\nDivision of labour separates a production process into tasks. Specialisation concentrates a person, firm or region on particular activities.\n\nEXAMPLE: In a garment factory, some workers cut fabric, others stitch, and others package finished clothes. Repetition can improve speed and skill.\n\nBenefits: higher productivity, reduced switching time, easier mechanisation, training in specialised skills and lower unit cost.\nCosts: monotony, worker dependence, disruption when one stage stops, difficulty changing occupations and loss of broad craft skills.\nLimits: market size (Adam Smith's famous observation), nature of production, capital, transport and labour skills.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Division of labour is a way of organising work; it is not automatically the same as the international division of labour between countries.\n\n4. PRODUCTIVITY, EFFICIENCY AND OUTPUT\nOutput is the total quantity produced. Labour productivity = output ÷ labour input, often output per worker or per hour.\nIf 5 workers make 100 chairs, output per worker is 20 chairs. If improved tools raise production to 125 with the same workforce, productivity becomes 25 chairs per worker, a 25% rise.\n\nHigher total output does NOT always mean higher productivity: hiring twice as many workers to produce only 50% more output reduces output per worker.\n\n5. SHORT RUN AND LONG RUN\nShort run: at least one factor of production is fixed.\nLong run: all factors can be varied.\nThese terms do not correspond to a universal number of months. For a roadside food vendor, buying another stove may be quick; for a steel plant, adding a production line may take years.\n\n6. TOTAL, AVERAGE AND MARGINAL PRODUCT\n• Total product (TP): all output from a given quantity of variable input.\n• Average product (AP) = TP ÷ number of units of variable input.\n• Marginal product (MP) = change in TP ÷ change in variable input.\n\nWORKED TABLE — WORKERS ON A FIXED FARM\nWorkers:           0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6\nTotal bags:        0 | 8 | 20| 33| 42| 48| 48\nMarginal bags:     – | 8 | 12| 13|  9|  6|  0\nAverage bags:      – | 8 | 10| 11|10.5|9.6| 8\n\nRead the table carefully:\n• MP initially rises from 8 to 12 to 13.\n• Diminishing marginal returns begin when MP falls from 13 to 9, on adding worker 4.\n• TP keeps rising as long as MP remains positive.\n• TP reaches its maximum at worker 6, where MP is zero.\n• AP reaches its maximum at worker 3 in this discrete example.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Diminishing returns begin when MP starts FALLING, not when MP becomes negative and not when TP begins falling.\n\n7. LAW OF VARIABLE PROPORTIONS\nHolding technology and some inputs fixed, increasing a variable input eventually yields diminishing marginal product. The law does not mean that adding any worker immediately reduces output.\n\nThree conventional stages:\n• Stage I: AP rises; fixed inputs are increasingly well utilised.\n• Stage II: AP falls but MP remains positive; TP continues to increase at a decreasing rate.\n• Stage III: MP is negative; adding labour reduces TP.\nA rational producer under the standard model typically operates in Stage II, though the exact choice depends on input and output prices.\n\n8. RETURNS TO SCALE — A DIFFERENT QUESTION\nIn the LONG RUN, vary ALL inputs by the same percentage:\n• Increasing returns to scale: inputs double, output more than doubles.\n• Constant returns: inputs double, output doubles.\n• Decreasing returns: inputs double, output less than doubles.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Doubling workers AND machines from 10 of each to 20 of each increases output from 100 to 250. Output rises by 150%, inputs by 100%: increasing returns to scale.\n\nDO NOT CONFUSE diminishing marginal returns (one input changes, others fixed) with decreasing returns to scale (all inputs change proportionately).\n\n9. ECONOMIES OF SCALE\nEconomies of scale reduce long-run average cost as production expands.\nINTERNAL economies arise within a growing firm: specialised machinery, bulk buying, management specialists, cheaper access to finance and spreading fixed administrative costs.\nEXTERNAL economies arise from the growth or clustering of an industry: skilled local labour, specialised suppliers, shared infrastructure and knowledge spillovers.\n\nDiseconomies can emerge from excessive size: coordination delays, management complexity, congestion and rising local input prices.\n\n10. LOCATION VS LOCALISATION\nLocation asks why ONE firm chooses a site. Localisation means firms in the SAME industry cluster in an area.\nLocation factors: proximity to bulky raw materials, customers, skilled workers, transport, energy, land, security, government policy and access to finance.\n\nNIGERIAN EXAMPLES: Cement production near limestone deposits such as Obajana and Ewekoro; commercial firms locating in Lagos to reach a large market; garment and leather-related enterprises clustering in established production centres.\n\nA cluster may offer shared suppliers and skills, but it can also create congestion and dependence on one industry.\n\n11. WHY SMALL FIRMS SURVIVE\nLarge firms do not always win. Small firms can offer personal services, flexible production, local convenience and specialised products. Barbers, repairers and neighbourhood food sellers may operate effectively without enormous factories.\n\n12. APPLIED JAMB SCENARIOS\nA factory adds workers while floor space and machinery stay unchanged. Which law? Variable proportions.\nA company doubles both labour and machinery, and output triples. Which result? Increasing returns to scale.\nA firm moves close to a quarry because stone is bulky and costly to transport. Which location factor? Raw materials.\nAn industry cluster attracts specialised component suppliers. Internal or external economy? External.\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. TP rises from 42 to 48 when the fifth worker is hired. MP of worker 5? 6 units.\n2. When MP is positive but falling, what happens to TP? It rises, but by smaller increments.\n3. What happens when MP becomes negative? TP falls.\n4. Why is a bank loan not automatically “capital” in the physical factor sense? It finances the purchase of productive assets; the loan is financial capital.\n5. Output rises 40% after all inputs rise 20%. Returns to scale? Increasing.\n6. What is the difference between localisation and location? Industry clustering versus siting one firm.\n7. If 8 workers produce 160 units, what is output per worker? 20 units.\n8. Which reward is conventionally assigned to entrepreneurship? Profit."}},{"id":"costs-revenue","old":{"title":"Costs, revenue and profit","body":"Cost of production is the expenditure on factors and inputs. Economists include opportunity costs (implicit costs), so normal profit is counted as a cost.\n\nShort-run costs:\n• Fixed cost (FC): does not change with output (rent, insurance, salaries of permanent staff); paid even at zero output.\n• Variable cost (VC): changes with output (raw materials, fuel, casual wages).\n• Total cost TC = FC + VC.\n• Average fixed cost AFC = FC ÷ Q (falls continuously).\n• Average variable cost AVC = VC ÷ Q.\n• Average (total) cost AC = TC ÷ Q = AFC + AVC.\n• Marginal cost MC = change in TC ÷ change in Q (the cost of one more unit).\n\nWorked example: fixed cost ₦200.\nOutput: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\nVC (₦): 0, 100, 180, 240, 320\nTC (₦): 200, 300, 380, 440, 520\nMC (₦): –, 100, 80, 60, 80\nAt Q = 4: AC = 520 ÷ 4 = ₦130; AVC = 320 ÷ 4 = ₦80; AFC = 200 ÷ 4 = ₦50 (₦80 + ₦50 = ₦130).\nThe AC, AVC and MC curves are U-shaped (because of variable proportions). MC cuts AVC and AC at their lowest points.\n\nLong run: all costs are variable. The long-run average cost curve is U-shaped (economies, then diseconomies of scale), an \"envelope\" of short-run AC curves.\n\nRevenue:\n• Total revenue TR = price × quantity.\n• Average revenue AR = TR ÷ Q = price (AR is the demand curve).\n• Marginal revenue MR = change in TR ÷ change in Q.\nUnder perfect competition P = AR = MR (horizontal). Under monopoly MR is below AR because the firm must lower price to sell more.\n\nProfit:\n• Profit = TR − TC.\n• Normal profit: the minimum profit to keep the entrepreneur in the industry (AR = AC); counted as a cost.\n• Supernormal (abnormal) profit: profit above normal (AR > AC).\n• Profit-maximising rule: produce where MC = MR (with MC rising).\n• Break-even point: TR = TC.\n• Short-run shutdown: a firm stays open if price covers AVC (it contributes to fixed cost); it shuts down if price < AVC. In the long run price must cover AC.\n\nBreak-even example: a firm has fixed cost ₦60,000, sells at ₦500 and has variable cost ₦300 per unit. Contribution per unit = ₦200; break-even output = 60,000 ÷ 200 = 300 units.\n\nCommon traps:\n• MC is the change in TOTAL cost, not in average cost; fixed cost does not affect MC.\n• AFC keeps falling as output rises; it is not U-shaped.\n• AC = AFC + AVC (do not add MC).\n• A loss-making firm can still produce in the short run if price ≥ AVC.\n• Normal profit is part of cost, so \"zero economic profit\" still pays the entrepreneur."},"new":{"title":"Costs, revenue and profit","body":"COSTS, REVENUE AND PROFIT — HOW FIRMS MAKE DECISIONS\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nJAMB frequently combines definitions with short calculations. Learn which cost changes with output, how to calculate marginal values and why a firm can rationally continue operating even while making a short-run loss.\n\n1. ACCOUNTING COST VS ECONOMIC COST\nExplicit costs are actual payments: wages, electricity bills and raw materials.\nImplicit costs are opportunity costs of resources owned by the firm: an owner's forgone salary or rent on a personally owned shop.\n\nAccounting profit = revenue − explicit costs.\nEconomic profit = revenue − (explicit + implicit costs).\n\nEXAMPLE: A shop earns ₦2 million and pays ₦1.4 million in bills. Accounting profit = ₦600,000. If the owner gave up a job paying ₦500,000, and this is the relevant additional implicit cost, economic profit = ₦100,000.\n\nNormal profit is the minimum return needed to keep entrepreneurial resources in their current use. It is included in economic costs. Zero ECONOMIC profit therefore does not mean the owner receives nothing.\n\n2. FIXED, VARIABLE AND TOTAL COST\n• Total fixed cost (TFC): does not vary with output in the relevant short run; rent on a leased factory is an example.\n• Total variable cost (TVC): changes with output, such as materials and piece-rate labour.\n• Total cost (TC) = TFC + TVC.\n\nAt zero output, a firm may still pay fixed costs. In the long run, all inputs can be varied, so there are no fixed costs in the same economic sense.\n\n3. AVERAGE AND MARGINAL COST\n• Average fixed cost (AFC) = TFC/Q.\n• Average variable cost (AVC) = TVC/Q.\n• Average total cost (ATC or AC) = TC/Q = AFC + AVC.\n• Marginal cost (MC) = change in TC ÷ change in output.\n\nWORKED TABLE — FIXED COST ₦300\nQuantity:           0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5\nVariable cost (₦):  0 |120|210|270|360|480\nTotal cost (₦):   300|420|510|570|660|780\nMarginal cost (₦): – |120| 90| 60| 90|120\n\nAt Q = 4:\nAFC = 300/4 = ₦75.\nAVC = 360/4 = ₦90.\nATC = 660/4 = ₦165.\nCheck: 75 + 90 = 165.\n\nMC of the fourth unit = 660 − 570 = ₦90. It is NOT 660/4.\n\n4. SHAPES OF COST CURVES\n• TFC is horizontal when plotted against output.\n• TVC generally rises as output rises.\n• AFC continuously falls as output rises; it is NOT U-shaped.\n• AVC and ATC are conventionally U-shaped in the short-run textbook model.\n• MC typically first falls and then rises due to changing marginal productivity.\n\nMC crosses AVC and ATC at their respective minimum points in the smooth textbook model. WHY? When marginal cost is below the current average, it pulls the average down; when above, it pulls the average up.\n\n5. LINK BETWEEN PRODUCTIVITY AND COST\nIf labour is the only variable input and its wage per unit is fixed, rising marginal product means less additional labour cost per extra unit of output: MC tends to fall. When diminishing marginal product sets in, MC tends to rise.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Diminishing marginal RETURNS and rising marginal COST are connected, but the former is measured in output and the latter in money.\n\n6. REVENUE: TOTAL, AVERAGE AND MARGINAL\n• Total revenue (TR) = price × quantity sold.\n• Average revenue (AR) = TR/Q; for a single-price firm, AR = selling price.\n• Marginal revenue (MR) = change in TR ÷ change in quantity.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE — A FIRM THAT MUST LOWER PRICE TO SELL MORE\nAt 3 units, price ₦500 → TR ₦1,500.\nAt 4 units, price ₦450 → TR ₦1,800.\nMR of the fourth unit = ₦1,800 − ₦1,500 = ₦300, not ₦450.\nThe firm earns ₦450 on the new unit but loses ₦50 on each of the three earlier units.\n\nUnder perfect competition, an individual firm takes price as given, so P = AR = MR. With a downward-sloping firm demand curve, MR is generally below AR.\n\n7. PROFIT MAXIMISATION\nProfit = TR − TC.\nThe conventional interior profit-maximising condition is MR = MC, with MC crossing MR in the appropriate direction (or profit falling for the next unit). Equality alone does not guarantee a maximum in every possible case.\n\nDISCRETE EXAMPLE\nAt Q = 4, TR = ₦1,000 and TC = ₦800 → profit ₦200.\nAt Q = 5, TR = ₦1,200 and TC = ₦950 → profit ₦250.\nAt Q = 6, TR = ₦1,350 and TC = ₦1,150 → profit ₦200.\nAmong these options, Q = 5 maximises profit.\n\n8. BREAK-EVEN AND CONTRIBUTION\nBreak-even means TR = TC: no accounting profit or loss under the relevant cost definition.\nFor a constant selling price and constant variable cost per unit:\nContribution per unit = selling price − variable cost per unit.\nBreak-even units = total fixed cost ÷ contribution per unit.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nFixed cost ₦90,000; price ₦800; variable cost ₦500 per unit.\nContribution = ₦300.\nBreak-even quantity = 90,000/300 = 300 units.\nAt 400 units, profit = 400(300) − 90,000 = ₦30,000.\n\nIf contribution is zero or negative, increasing sales alone cannot recover fixed cost under this simple model.\n\n9. THE SHORT-RUN SHUTDOWN DECISION\nA perfectly competitive firm compares market price with AVC at the relevant output:\n• If price exceeds AVC, operating may contribute toward fixed costs even if ATC is not fully covered.\n• If price is below minimum AVC, shutdown is preferable in the standard short-run model.\n• At the minimum-AVC shutdown point, the firm is indifferent in the simplified model.\n\nEXAMPLE: Selling price ₦70, AVC ₦60, ATC ₦90. The firm loses ₦20 per unit on a total-cost basis, but earns ₦10 per unit toward fixed cost. Shutting down would leave fixed costs uncovered. Operating may reduce the loss.\n\nThis rule assumes avoidable variable costs, no other shutdown costs or constraints, and the standard competitive setting.\n\n10. LONG-RUN AVERAGE COST\nAs a firm expands its scale, internal economies may lower long-run average cost. Eventually management difficulties or other diseconomies may raise it. The conventional long-run AC curve is U-shaped, though real industries may have flatter or more complex patterns.\n\n11. COMMON EXAM MIX-UPS\nMC ≠ average cost.\nFixed cost ≠ sunk cost: some fixed costs can be avoided by closing in the long run.\nAR = price for a single-price firm; MR need not equal price.\nZero economic profit ≠ zero accounting profit.\nBreak-even ≠ maximum profit.\nProfit ≠ revenue.\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. TFC ₦200, TVC ₦600, Q = 4. ATC? (200 + 600)/4 = ₦200.\n2. TC rises from ₦500 to ₦580 as Q rises from 4 to 5. MC? ₦80.\n3. Price ₦500, variable cost ₦350, fixed cost ₦30,000. Break-even output? 200 units.\n4. Why can a firm produce at a short-run loss? Revenue may cover variable cost and some fixed cost.\n5. Does normal profit count as economic cost? Yes.\n6. What happens to AFC as output increases? It falls.\n7. In perfect competition, what is the relationship between price and MR? They are equal.\n8. A firm earns ₦2,000 TR and incurs ₦1,750 TC. Profit? ₦250."}},{"id":"market-structures","old":{"title":"Market structures","body":"A market is any arrangement by which buyers and sellers exchange goods and services; it need not be a place. Market structures are classed by the number of firms, the type of product, entry barriers and control over price.\n\nPerfect competition:\n• Many buyers and sellers, homogeneous product, free entry and exit, perfect knowledge, perfect mobility of factors, no transport costs.\n• Each firm is a price-taker: its demand curve is horizontal and P = AR = MR.\n• Short run: may earn supernormal profit or loss. Long run: free entry and exit leave only normal profit (P = AC at its minimum), so it is efficient.\n• Close real examples: markets for grains, tomatoes, or garri in a big open market.\n\nMonopoly:\n• A single seller of a product with no close substitute; strong barriers to entry.\n• Sources: control of raw materials, patents and copyrights, legal grant (statute), huge economies of scale (natural monopoly), mergers and cartels.\n• Price-maker: downward-sloping demand (AR) with MR below it. Equilibrium at MC = MR; price read from AR. Can keep supernormal profit in the long run.\n• Disadvantages: higher prices, lower output, inefficiency, exploitation; advantages: economies of scale, funds for research.\n• Nigerian examples from the past: NEPA (electricity) and NITEL (telephones). Both have since been privatised or replaced: NEPA became PHCN (2005) and was unbundled into GenCos and DisCos handed to private owners in 2013; telecoms were liberalised from 2001 (MTN, Airtel, Glo) and NITEL was sold off.\n\nPrice discrimination: a monopolist charges different prices to different buyers for the same good, for reasons not related to cost (e.g. doctors charging rich and poor patients differently; day and night tariffs). Conditions: the seller has monopoly power, markets can be separated (no resale), and elasticities of demand differ. A higher price is charged where demand is less elastic.\n\nMonopolistic competition:\n• Many firms, differentiated products (brands), fairly free entry: hairdressers, restaurants, bakeries.\n• Each firm has a downward-sloping demand curve; uses advertising. Supernormal profit is competed away in the long run, leaving excess capacity.\n\nOligopoly:\n• A few large firms, interdependent; products may be identical (cement) or differentiated (telecoms: MTN, Airtel, Glo, 9mobile).\n• Prices tend to be rigid (kinked demand curve); firms compete through advertising and may collude to form cartels (OPEC behaves like one).\n• Duopoly: two firms.\n\nMonopsony: a single buyer.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Under monopoly, price is above MR; profit is maximised at MC = MR, not at the highest price.\n• Supernormal profit disappears in the long run under perfect and monopolistic competition, not under monopoly.\n• Product differentiation marks monopolistic competition, not perfect competition.\n• Price discrimination needs DIFFERENT elasticities in separable markets.\n• A natural monopoly comes from economies of scale, not from a law."},"new":{"title":"Market structures","body":"MARKET STRUCTURES — WHO COMPETES, WHO SETS PRICE?\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nMarkets differ in the number of sellers, product similarity, ease of entry and firms' influence over price. JAMB frequently asks you to identify a structure from its characteristics, not merely recall its name.\n\n1. WHAT IS A MARKET?\nA market is an arrangement enabling buyers and sellers to exchange goods or services. It can be physical, such as a grain market, or digital. A market structure describes the competitive environment in which firms operate.\n\nFOUR QUESTIONS TO CLASSIFY A MARKET\n• How many important sellers are there?\n• Are products identical or differentiated?\n• Can new firms enter easily?\n• How much control does each seller have over price?\n\n2. PERFECT COMPETITION\nThe ideal textbook conditions include:\n• Many small buyers and sellers.\n• Homogeneous products.\n• Free entry and exit.\n• Good market information.\n• Individual firms unable to influence market price.\n\nA competitive firm is a PRICE TAKER. Its own demand curve is horizontal at the prevailing market price, so P = AR = MR.\n\nIMPORTANT: The entire MARKET demand curve slopes downward. It is the individual competitive FIRM'S demand curve that is horizontal. Confusing the two is a classic exam error.\n\nSHORT RUN: A firm can earn supernormal profit, normal profit or a loss.\nLONG RUN: With free entry and exit and standard assumptions, economic profit is competed down to zero (normal profit), with P = minimum ATC for a representative firm.\n\nReal agricultural markets may approximate some features but rarely satisfy every assumption perfectly.\n\n3. MONOPOLY\nA monopoly is a market with one seller of a product lacking close substitutes, protected by significant entry barriers.\n\nBarriers may include patents, control of essential inputs, legal restrictions, network effects and very large economies of scale.\n\nA monopolist faces the market demand curve, so AR slopes downward and MR lies below AR for a single-price seller with ordinary downward-sloping demand.\n\nPROFIT MAXIMISATION\nChoose output where MR = MC under the standard interior conditions, then read the price from the DEMAND/AR curve at that output.\n\nJAMB TRAP: Monopoly does not mean the firm can charge any price it wants. Higher prices generally reduce quantity demanded, and buyers' alternatives and purchasing power matter.\n\nA monopoly may sustain supernormal profit in the long run because barriers restrict entry, but supernormal profit is not guaranteed.\n\n4. NATURAL MONOPOLY\nA natural monopoly arises when one provider can supply the relevant market at a lower cost than multiple competing networks because of strong economies of scale or scope. Water distribution networks are a common illustration.\n\nA natural monopoly is defined by COST conditions, not simply by a law declaring a firm the sole provider.\n\nHISTORICAL NIGERIAN CONTEXT: NEPA and NITEL are often cited in older exam questions as state monopolies. They are historical examples, not accurate descriptions of the current organisation of Nigeria's electricity and telecommunications sectors.\n\n5. MONOPOLISTIC COMPETITION\nMany sellers offer DIFFERENTIATED products that are close but imperfect substitutes. Entry is relatively easy.\n\nExamples: restaurants, salons, bakeries and clothing brands.\nFirms compete through quality, branding, convenience and advertising as well as price.\n\nBecause each brand has some customer loyalty, its demand curve slopes downward. In the long-run textbook equilibrium, free entry tends to eliminate supernormal profit, but firms may operate with excess capacity.\n\nDO NOT CONFUSE: “Monopolistic” here does NOT mean one seller. It means each firm has limited monopoly power over its differentiated product.\n\n6. OLIGOPOLY\nAn oligopoly has a FEW strategically interdependent major firms. Each must consider rivals' likely reactions before changing price, output or advertising.\n\nProducts may be homogeneous (some bulk materials) or differentiated (telecommunications services).\n\nPossible behaviour:\n• Price leadership: a prominent firm moves first and rivals follow.\n• Non-price competition: service quality, advertising, loyalty programmes.\n• Collusion: rivals coordinate prices or output, potentially harming consumers.\n• Price wars: rivals repeatedly cut prices.\n\nThe kinked-demand model is ONE explanation of price rigidity, not a universal law governing every oligopoly.\n\n7. DUOPOLY, MONOPSONY AND OLIGOPSONY\n• Duopoly: two major sellers.\n• Monopsony: one buyer in a defined market.\n• Oligopsony: a few major buyers.\n\nEXAMPLE: If one processing plant is the only buyer of a particular crop in a remote district, farmers may face local monopsony power even if many buyers exist nationally.\n\nSeller concentration and buyer concentration are different concepts.\n\n8. PRICE DISCRIMINATION\nPrice discrimination occurs when a seller charges different prices for comparable units of a product in ways not fully explained by differences in cost.\n\nConditions often include:\n• Some market power.\n• Ability to distinguish customers or transactions.\n• Limited resale or arbitrage.\n• Different willingness to pay or demand responsiveness.\n\nEXAMPLE: Different ticket prices for students and adults may be price discrimination if the price difference exceeds any cost difference.\n\nWith third-degree price discrimination, the seller generally charges a HIGHER markup in the market with LESS elastic demand, other things equal.\n\nNot every price difference is discrimination: a delivery charge reflecting higher transport cost may simply reflect different costs.\n\n9. COMPARISON TABLE IN WORDS\nPerfect competition: many firms; identical products; easy entry; price takers.\nMonopolistic competition: many firms; differentiated products; relatively easy entry; some price discretion.\nOligopoly: few firms; identical or differentiated products; substantial entry barriers; strategic interdependence.\nMonopoly: one seller; no close substitutes; high barriers; substantial price discretion.\n\n10. MARKET POWER AND PUBLIC POLICY\nGovernments may regulate utilities, investigate cartels, protect consumers or promote entry. Competition can encourage lower prices and innovation, but a single network may sometimes be cheaper to operate than several duplicate networks.\n\nDistinguish the goal of protecting COMPETITION from protecting every existing competitor.\n\n11. JAMB TRAPS — DECIDE FROM THE FEATURES\n“Many firms selling slightly different soaps” → monopolistic competition.\n“One firm with legal exclusivity and no close substitute” → monopoly.\n“A few cement producers reacting to each other's prices” → oligopoly.\n“Many sellers of identical produce, each unable to affect price” → approximately perfect competition.\n“Only one major employer buying labour in a town” → monopsony.\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Under perfect competition, what equals MR? Market price and AR.\n2. Is a monopolist's MR above or below AR? Below AR under ordinary downward-sloping demand.\n3. What is the defining strategic feature of oligopoly? Interdependence among a few major firms.\n4. Why can monopolistic competitors charge slightly different prices? Product differentiation.\n5. What is a duopoly? Two major sellers.\n6. Does a natural monopoly require a government decree? No; it arises from cost conditions.\n7. What does long-run zero economic profit mean in perfect competition? The firm earns normal profit.\n8. Which market structure is defined by one buyer? Monopsony."}},{"id":"business-organisations","old":{"title":"Business organisations","body":"Business organisations are the forms in which firms are owned and run. Main forms in Nigeria:\n\n1. Sole proprietorship (one-man business): owned and controlled by one person, e.g. a provision store or a barber's shop.\n• Advantages: easy to start, owner keeps all profit, quick decisions, personal contact, secrecy.\n• Disadvantages: unlimited liability, limited capital, no continuity at death, owner must do everything.\n\n2. Partnership: 2 to 20 persons (the usual legal limit for ordinary partnerships) carry on business together, guided by a partnership deed. Types: ordinary (general) partnership, limited partnership (at least one general partner with unlimited liability), sleeping (dormant) partner who provides capital without taking part in management, and nominal partner who lends only his name. CAMA 2020 also introduced the limited liability partnership (LLP).\n• Advantages: more capital and skills; disadvantages: unlimited liability for general partners, disagreements, dissolution on death or withdrawal.\n\n3. Joint stock companies (limited liability companies), registered with the Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC) under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA) 2020.\n• Private limited company (Ltd): shares are not offered to the public and cannot be freely transferred; since CAMA 2020 one person can form one (formerly at least 2).\n• Public limited company (Plc): minimum of 2 members, no maximum; can sell shares to the public and be listed on the Nigerian Exchange (NGX).\n• Documents: memorandum of association (external: name, objects, capital) and articles of association (internal rules). A certificate of incorporation gives separate legal personality; a public company also issues a prospectus.\n• Advantages: limited liability, large capital, continuity (perpetual succession). Disadvantages: costly to form, separation of ownership and control, double taxation, disclosure.\n\n4. Cooperative societies: owned by members for mutual benefit, one member one vote, profit shared as dividend on purchases or patronage. Types: producers' (farmers' marketing), consumers' (retail), credit and thrift (savings and loans), and multipurpose.\n\n5. Public enterprises (corporations and parastatals): owned by government and set up by law, e.g. the former NEPA, NITEL, Nigerian Railway Corporation and NNPC. Aims: provide essential services, control strategic industries, prevent exploitation.\nProblems: political interference, inefficiency, corruption, losses and dependence on subsidy.\n\nPrivatisation and commercialisation: under SAP, the Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation (TCPC) was set up in 1988; the Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE) has handled it since 1999 (Public Enterprises (Privatisation and Commercialisation) Act 1999). Privatisation = selling government shares to private owners (power DisCos/GenCos 2013, NITEL). Commercialisation = making a public enterprise run on commercial lines and cover its costs without subsidy (NNPC became NNPC Limited in 2022 under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021).\n\nSources of finance: owners' savings, ploughed-back profit, shares (equity) and debentures (loans to a company with fixed interest), bank loans and overdrafts, trade credit, hire purchase, leasing, government grants.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Debenture holders are creditors (get interest); shareholders are owners (get dividends).\n• Limited liability means owners lose only what they invested.\n• A sleeping partner contributes capital but takes no part in management.\n• Commercialisation keeps government ownership; privatisation transfers ownership.\n• The memorandum governs the company's dealings with outsiders; the articles govern internal affairs."},"new":{"title":"Business organisations","body":"BUSINESS ORGANISATIONS — OWNERSHIP, CONTROL AND FINANCE\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nA business's legal form affects who owns it, who makes decisions, who bears debts, how funds are raised and whether it survives changes in membership. JAMB often contrasts unlimited liability with limited liability and privatisation with commercialisation.\n\n1. SOLE PROPRIETORSHIP\nOne individual owns and controls the enterprise, usually receiving its profit and bearing its risks.\n\nEXAMPLE: A neighbourhood tailoring shop run by its owner.\n\nAdvantages: simple formation, quick decisions, privacy, direct customer contact and owner motivation.\nDisadvantages: limited capital, dependence on one person's skill and time, potentially difficult continuity and UNLIMITED liability in an ordinary unincorporated sole proprietorship.\n\nUNLIMITED LIABILITY means business debts can expose the owner's personal assets, subject to applicable law. It does not mean the owner has unlimited money.\n\n2. PARTNERSHIP\nTwo or more persons carry on business together for profit. The legal form matters: an ordinary/general partnership differs from a limited partnership or a limited liability partnership (LLP).\n\nA partnership agreement or deed may specify capital contributions, profit sharing, duties, admission and retirement of partners, and dispute resolution.\n\n• General partner: typically participates in management and bears unlimited liability in an ordinary partnership.\n• Limited partner: may enjoy limited liability subject to the legal rules of the limited-partnership structure.\n• Sleeping/dormant partner: contributes resources but does not take part in daily management; being “sleeping” does NOT automatically remove liability.\n• LLP: a separate registered structure providing limited liability subject to its governing law.\n\nIMPORTANT LEGAL CAUTION: Old textbooks often state a general 2–20 membership limit. Nigerian business law has evolved, including CAMA 2020 and LLP structures; avoid treating a textbook number as a universal rule for every partnership form.\n\nAdvantages: pooled finance and skills, shared workload and potentially better decisions.\nDisadvantages: disagreements, shared profits and liability risks depending on the structure.\n\n3. PRIVATE LIMITED COMPANY\nA registered company has a legal identity separate from its shareholders. Its shares are not offered to the general public in the manner of a public company, and transfer restrictions may apply.\n\nUnder CAMA 2020, a private company can be formed by one person, subject to applicable requirements.\n\nLIMITED LIABILITY generally means a shareholder's exposure for company debts is limited to the unpaid amount on shares or the applicable guarantee; personal guarantees, misconduct or other exceptions can create additional liability.\n\nSeparate legal personality also means the company can own property and enter contracts in its own name.\n\n4. PUBLIC LIMITED COMPANY\nA public company may raise equity from the public subject to securities regulation and company-law requirements. A public company is not necessarily LISTED on the Nigerian Exchange: listing is a separate step.\n\nEXAMPLE: A large manufacturing firm might become a public company to access a broader investor base.\n\nAdvantages: greater ability to raise capital, continuity, transferable shares and risk sharing.\nDisadvantages: disclosure and compliance costs, possible separation of ownership and management, and pressure from diverse shareholders.\n\n5. SHAREHOLDERS VS DEBENTURE HOLDERS\nShareholders are owners. They may receive dividends when declared and have rights according to their share class.\nDebenture holders are creditors: the company owes them principal and contractual interest.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nA company issues shares worth ₦5 million and debentures worth ₦3 million.\nThe ₦5 million represents equity finance; the ₦3 million represents borrowed finance.\nA dividend is not the same thing as loan interest. A company may owe debenture interest even when it pays no dividend, subject to the terms of the instrument.\n\n6. DOCUMENTS AND REGISTRATION\nThe Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC) registers companies under Nigerian company law. A certificate of incorporation is evidence of registration.\n\nThe memorandum and articles of association form part of the constitutional documentation of companies under the relevant legal framework. Older exam materials commonly describe the memorandum as covering external matters and articles as internal management rules; modern company-law practice is more nuanced, so learn the textbook distinction without assuming every historical rule remains unchanged.\n\nA prospectus is a disclosure document associated with certain public securities offers; it is NOT automatically required merely because a company exists.\n\n7. COOPERATIVE SOCIETIES\nCooperatives are member-centred enterprises organised to meet shared economic or social needs.\n\nTypes:\n• Consumer cooperative: members obtain goods at favourable terms.\n• Producer/marketing cooperative: farmers or producers pool marketing or resources.\n• Credit and thrift cooperative: members save and obtain loans.\n• Multipurpose cooperative: combines activities.\n\nCooperative governance commonly follows democratic member control, often expressed as “one member, one vote”, subject to the cooperative's rules and applicable law.\n\nA cooperative's central aim is MEMBER BENEFIT, not necessarily maximising profits for outside shareholders.\n\n8. PUBLIC ENTERPRISES\nPublic enterprises are organisations owned or controlled by government to provide goods or services, manage strategic assets or meet public objectives.\n\nPotential benefits: essential service provision, investment in large infrastructure and pursuit of social goals.\nCommon problems: political interference, weak incentives, inadequate funding, poor accountability and inefficient operations.\n\nDo not assume every publicly owned enterprise is automatically inefficient, or that private ownership always solves service-delivery problems.\n\n9. PRIVATISATION VS COMMERCIALISATION\nPrivatisation transfers some or all government ownership to private investors.\nCommercialisation changes how an enterprise operates, emphasising revenue, cost recovery and commercial discipline; ownership need not change.\n\nEXAMPLE: Selling government shares in an enterprise is privatisation. Requiring a state-owned enterprise to cover operating costs through user charges, while keeping state ownership, is commercialisation.\n\nNIGERIAN CONTEXT\nNigeria pursued major public-enterprise reforms from the late 1980s. The Bureau of Public Enterprises has played a central role in later programmes. The power-sector restructuring and NNPC's transition to NNPC Limited are distinct developments; corporatisation is not necessarily identical to a sale of government ownership.\n\n10. SOURCES OF BUSINESS FINANCE\nINTERNAL: owners' savings, retained profits and sale of surplus assets.\nEXTERNAL: share issues, debentures, bank loans, overdrafts, trade credit, leasing, hire purchase and grants.\n\nShort-term finance typically supports working capital such as stock and receivables; long-term finance is often used for buildings and machinery.\n\n• Overdraft: permission to draw beyond a bank-account balance within agreed limits.\n• Trade credit: suppliers permit payment later.\n• Hire purchase: instalment arrangement with ownership transfer subject to the contract.\n• Leasing: use of an asset in exchange for periodic payments, without necessarily acquiring ownership.\n\n11. BUSINESS COMBINATIONS\nHorizontal integration: firms at the SAME production stage combine, such as two competing bakeries.\nVertical integration: firms at DIFFERENT stages combine, such as a flour mill acquiring a bakery.\nConglomerate integration: firms in unrelated activities combine.\n\nBackward integration moves toward suppliers; forward integration moves toward distribution or customers.\n\n12. JAMB DECISION GUIDE\nOne owner + unlimited liability → sole proprietorship.\nOwners share management and profit → partnership, subject to its legal form.\nSeparate legal personality + shares → incorporated company.\nMembers cooperate for shared benefit → cooperative.\nGovernment ownership + public service objectives → public enterprise.\nGovernment sells ownership → privatisation.\nGovernment retains ownership but demands commercial operation → commercialisation.\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. Who owns a company: shareholders or debenture holders? Shareholders.\n2. Does “sleeping partner” necessarily mean limited liability? No.\n3. Can a private company have one member under CAMA 2020? Yes, subject to requirements.\n4. Is every public company listed on the stock exchange? No.\n5. What distinguishes privatisation from commercialisation? Ownership transfer versus operating approach.\n6. What is backward integration? Acquiring or controlling suppliers.\n7. Which financing method allows buying goods now and paying the supplier later? Trade credit.\n8. What is a central cooperative objective? Mutual member benefit."}},{"id":"distributive-trade","old":{"title":"Distributive trade","body":"Distribution is the movement of goods from producers to final consumers. It completes production. Trade is the buying and selling of goods: home trade (wholesale and retail) and foreign trade (import, export, entrepôt).\n\nChannels of distribution:\n• Producer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer (the traditional chain, e.g. soft drinks, detergents).\n• Producer → retailer → consumer (large retailers such as supermarkets buy direct).\n• Producer → consumer (direct: farm gate sales, bakeries, mail order, online sales, a tailor).\n• Producer → agent → wholesaler → retailer → consumer (imports and some farm produce).\nChoice of channel depends on the nature of the product (perishable, technical), size of the market, the producer's finance, and the location of consumers.\n\nWholesaler: buys in bulk from producers and sells in smaller quantities to retailers.\nFunctions to the producer: buys in bulk, finances production by paying promptly, stores goods, bears risk, gives market information. To the retailer: breaks bulk, offers credit, a wide range of goods, delivery, advice. Some wholesalers grade, pack and brand goods.\nArguments for eliminating the wholesaler: producers and large retailers can do the functions themselves, and the wholesaler's profit raises prices. But the functions must still be done by someone.\n\nRetailer: the last link, selling in small quantities to final consumers.\nFunctions: breaks bulk, stocks a variety of goods, gives credit and after-sales service, delivers, informs producers of consumer tastes.\nTypes in Nigeria: hawkers and itinerant traders, market stalls, kiosks, mobile shops, unit shops, chain stores (multiple shops), department stores, supermarkets (e.g. Shoprite, which left Nigeria in 2021 under new local ownership; SPAR, Justrite), mail order and e-commerce (Jumia, Konga), and vending machines.\n\nMiddlemen: wholesalers, retailers, agents, brokers and commission agents who link producers and consumers. Criticised for hoarding, raising prices and artificial scarcity, but they provide useful services.\n\nAids to trade: transport, communication, banking, insurance, warehousing, advertising.\n\nProblems of distribution in Nigeria: poor roads and rail, inadequate storage (post-harvest losses of perishable produce), hoarding and smuggling, too many middlemen, poor finance, insecurity, multiple levies on roads.\n\nMarketing boards: commodity boards (Cocoa Marketing Board 1947, later Groundnut, Palm Produce and Cotton boards; restructured into commodity boards in 1977) bought export crops at fixed prices, graded and exported them, and stabilised farmers' incomes. They were criticised for paying farmers low prices and were abolished in 1986 under SAP.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Breaking bulk is done by both wholesalers (for retailers) and retailers (for consumers).\n• Removing the wholesaler does not remove his functions.\n• A chain store is many branches of one firm; a department store is one large shop with many departments.\n• Marketing boards were scrapped in 1986 (SAP), not in the 1960s.\n• Distribution is part of production, since production ends only when goods reach the consumer."},"new":{"title":"Distributive trade","body":"DISTRIBUTIVE TRADE — HOW GOODS REACH BUYERS\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS\nA product has little economic value to a consumer who cannot obtain it. Distribution links producers and users through transport, storage, wholesalers, retailers and digital channels. JAMB tests channels of distribution, functions of middlemen, trade terminology and Nigeria's distribution challenges.\n\n1. DISTRIBUTION AS PART OF ECONOMIC ACTIVITY\nDistribution makes goods available in the right place, at the right time and in suitable quantities. A farmer who produces tomatoes must still get them to buyers before they spoil.\n\nDistinguish:\n• Production: creating goods and services or adding utility.\n• Distribution: moving goods and services through channels to users.\n• Trade: buying and selling goods and services.\n• Commerce: trade plus activities that facilitate trade, such as banking, transport, insurance, communication and warehousing.\n\nSome introductory textbooks say production is not complete until goods reach the final consumer; the important idea is that distribution creates additional utility.\n\n2. HOME TRADE AND FOREIGN TRADE\nHome/internal trade takes place within a country.\nForeign/international trade crosses national borders.\n\n• Import: goods or services bought from abroad.\n• Export: goods or services sold abroad.\n• Entrepôt/re-export trade: importing goods and then exporting them again, often with limited processing, subject to the trade classification.\n\nEXAMPLE: A Nigerian merchant buys machinery from abroad: import. A cocoa exporter sells to a buyer overseas: export.\n\n3. CHANNELS OF DISTRIBUTION\nCommon channels:\nA. Producer → Consumer.\nB. Producer → Retailer → Consumer.\nC. Producer → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\nD. Producer → Agent → Wholesaler → Retailer → Consumer.\n\nEXAMPLES\nA tailor makes a shirt and sells directly to a customer: A.\nA factory supplies a supermarket, which sells to households: B.\nA detergent manufacturer supplies wholesalers, who supply neighbourhood shops: C.\n\nNo single channel is best for every product.\n\n4. WHAT DETERMINES THE CHANNEL?\n• Perishability: fresh fish and vegetables may need faster, shorter channels and cold storage.\n• Bulk and weight: heavy products may require specialised transport.\n• Market size and geographic spread: widely scattered buyers may need intermediaries.\n• Producer's resources: a firm with its own delivery network may sell directly.\n• Technical complexity: specialised equipment may need trained agents and after-sales service.\n• Consumer buying habits and digital access.\n\nA shorter channel is not automatically cheaper: someone must still perform transport, storage, marketing and delivery.\n\n5. WHOLESALERS — BULK BUYERS AND DISTRIBUTORS\nA wholesaler usually buys large quantities from producers or major suppliers and resells in smaller lots to retailers or other businesses.\n\nFunctions to producers:\n• Bulk purchases, reducing the number of transactions.\n• Storage and inventory holding.\n• Financing through advance or prompt payments.\n• Market information and access to dispersed buyers.\n• Bearing some transport and inventory risks.\n\nFunctions to retailers:\n• Breaking bulk into manageable quantities.\n• Supplying variety from different producers.\n• Providing trade credit where available.\n• Delivery, advice and market information.\n\nEXAMPLE: A beverage producer may sell a truckload to a wholesaler, who supplies dozens of small shops. The wholesaler performs logistics the producer would otherwise need to organise.\n\n6. RETAILERS — THE FINAL COMMERCIAL LINK\nRetailers sell to final consumers, usually in smaller quantities.\n\nFunctions:\n• Stocking goods near consumers.\n• Breaking bulk for household purchases.\n• Providing information and sometimes credit.\n• Offering convenience, delivery and after-sales service.\n• Communicating consumer preferences back through the supply chain.\n\nTypes:\n• Hawkers and mobile vendors.\n• Market stalls and neighbourhood shops.\n• Supermarkets and department stores.\n• Chain stores with multiple branches.\n• Online retailers and direct-to-consumer platforms.\n\nJAMB TRAP: A department store has many departments in one large retail establishment; a chain store has multiple branches under common ownership.\n\n7. MIDDLEMEN — NECESSARY OR UNNECESSARY?\nMiddlemen include wholesalers, retailers, agents and brokers. They can reduce search costs, aggregate goods, arrange transport, store stock and connect buyers with sellers.\n\nCriticisms: additional markups, possible hoarding, excessive layers, weak transparency and exploitative practices in poorly competitive markets.\n\nTHE IMPORTANT DISTINCTION: Eliminating a middleman does NOT eliminate the economic functions. A manufacturer selling directly must still advertise, process orders, store inventory and deliver products.\n\n8. AGENTS, BROKERS AND COMMISSION\nAn agent acts on behalf of another party, often helping to arrange transactions. A broker connects buyers and sellers and may receive a fee or commission. Ownership of the goods does not necessarily pass to the agent or broker.\n\nA wholesaler typically buys and resells goods on its own account; an agent may arrange a sale without taking title.\n\nEXAMPLE: A commission agent finds a buyer for a producer's goods and earns 5% of the sale value. On a ₦200,000 transaction, commission = 0.05 × 200,000 = ₦10,000.\n\n9. AIDS TO TRADE\n• Transport: moves goods and people.\n• Communication: conveys orders, prices and information.\n• Banking and payment systems: finance and settle transactions.\n• Insurance: transfers specified risks for a premium.\n• Warehousing: stores goods between production and sale.\n• Advertising: informs and persuades potential buyers.\n\nEach reduces a different obstacle to exchange.\n\n10. PROBLEMS OF DISTRIBUTION IN NIGERIA\n• Poor road links and high transport costs.\n• Inadequate cold chains and storage for perishable produce.\n• Insecurity and disruption of transport routes.\n• Multiple levies and regulatory bottlenecks.\n• Limited affordable finance for traders.\n• Weak market information and unpredictable supply.\n\nNIGERIAN EXAMPLE: Tomatoes can be plentiful near a producing area while expensive in a distant city because of spoilage, transport expenses and delays. The price difference is not explained solely by farm output.\n\n11. MARKETING BOARDS — HISTORICAL EXAM CONTEXT\nCommodity marketing boards historically purchased, graded and marketed major agricultural exports such as cocoa and groundnuts, often using administered producer prices.\n\nPotential aims: organised export marketing, quality control and stabilisation of producer earnings.\nCriticisms: low prices paid to farmers, administrative costs and government capture of export proceeds.\n\nNigeria's major commodity marketing boards were abolished in the mid-1980s, commonly dated to 1986 in JAMB-oriented accounts, during structural adjustment reforms. Do not describe them as currently operating in their old form.\n\n12. DIGITAL DISTRIBUTION\nOnline marketplaces allow producers and retailers to reach buyers without some traditional intermediaries, but payment processors, delivery firms, warehouses and platform operators may become NEW intermediaries.\n\nA digital order does not eliminate physical distribution for a physical product.\n\n13. EXAM TRAPS\nWholesaler sells mainly to retailers/businesses; retailer sells mainly to final consumers.\nAgent arranging sales ≠ wholesaler buying goods for resale.\nBreaking bulk can be done at more than one distribution stage.\nTrade ≠ commerce; commerce includes aids to trade.\nDirect selling ≠ no distribution cost.\nEntrepôt trade ≠ ordinary domestic trade.\n\nREVISION QUESTIONS WITH ANSWERS\n1. A factory sells to a wholesaler, then a shop sells to a household. Identify the channel. Producer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer.\n2. Who typically sells in small quantities to final consumers? Retailer.\n3. Does removing wholesalers remove warehousing and transport needs? No.\n4. A broker earns 4% commission on ₦250,000. How much? ₦10,000.\n5. Why might fresh fish use a short distribution channel? Perishability and need for speed/cold storage.\n6. What is the difference between trade and commerce? Commerce includes trade and its facilitating services.\n7. What is re-export trade? Importing goods and exporting them again.\n8. Give two distribution challenges in Nigeria. For example poor roads and inadequate cold storage."}},{"id":"national-income","old":{"title":"National income","body":"National income is the total money value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a year.\n\nConcepts:\n• Gross Domestic Product (GDP): value of output produced within the country's borders, by citizens and foreigners.\n• Gross National Product (GNP) = GDP + net factor income from abroad (income Nigerians earn abroad minus income foreigners earn in Nigeria). For Nigeria this is usually negative because of profits repatriated by foreign oil firms, so GNP < GDP.\n• Net National Product (NNP) = GNP − depreciation (capital consumption).\n• National income (NNP at factor cost) = NNP at market prices − indirect taxes + subsidies.\n• Per capita income = national income ÷ population.\n• Nominal (money) vs real income: real GDP removes the effect of price changes (using a price index/deflator).\n\nMeasurement:\n• Output (product) method: sum of value added at each stage, to avoid double counting.\n• Income method: wages + rent + interest + profit (exclude transfer payments).\n• Expenditure method: GDP = C + I + G + (X − M).\n\nWorked example (₦ billion): C = 600, I = 150, G = 200, X = 120, M = 170, net factor income from abroad = −30, depreciation = 70, indirect taxes = 60, subsidies = 10.\nStep 1: GDP at market prices = 600 + 150 + 200 + (120 − 170) = ₦900bn.\nStep 2: GNP = 900 − 30 = ₦870bn.\nStep 3: NNP = 870 − 70 = ₦800bn.\nStep 4: National income (factor cost) = 800 − 60 + 10 = ₦750bn.\n\nUses: measuring living standards and growth, comparing countries, planning, policy. Problems: illiteracy and poor records, the large informal (subsistence) sector, double counting, choice of prices, transfer payments, depreciation estimates, non-marketed services (housework). Nigeria's NBS rebased GDP in 2014 (base year 2010), making Nigeria Africa's largest economy at the time, and rebased again in 2025 (base year 2019).\n\nCircular flow: households supply factors to firms and receive income; firms sell goods to households. Injections: investment (I), government spending (G), exports (X). Withdrawals (leakages): savings (S), taxes (T), imports (M). Income is in equilibrium when injections = withdrawals (two-sector: S = I).\n\nConsumption, saving and investment:\n• Y = C + S. Average propensity to consume APC = C ÷ Y; marginal propensity to consume MPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY; MPC + MPS = 1.\n• Consumption function C = a + bY (a = autonomous consumption, b = MPC).\n• Investment depends on the interest rate, expected profit (marginal efficiency of capital), and confidence.\n\nMultiplier k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS. ΔY = k × ΔI.\nExample: MPC = 0.8, so MPS = 0.2 and k = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5. A ₦20bn rise in investment raises income by 5 × ₦20bn = ₦100bn.\n\nCommon traps:\n• GNP adds NET factor income from abroad; it can be smaller than GDP.\n• Depreciation turns gross into net; indirect taxes turn market price into factor cost (subtract taxes, add subsidies).\n• Transfer payments (pensions, gifts) are not part of national income.\n• The multiplier uses the MPS (or 1 − MPC), never the MPC alone.\n• Count only final goods, or value added, to avoid double counting."},"new":{"title":"National income","body":"NATIONAL INCOME — HOW AN ECONOMY'S OUTPUT AND INCOME ARE MEASURED\n\n1. MEANING AND THE CIRCULAR FLOW\nNational income measures the incomes earned by factors of production from current production during a period, usually one year. GDP measures the market value of FINAL goods and services produced within a country's borders during that period. GDP and national income are related but not identical: depreciation, income flows across borders and the difference between market prices and factor costs matter.\n\nImagine a Nigerian farmer selling cassava to a processor, who sells flour to a bakery, which sells bread to households. Adding every sale would count the same cassava repeatedly. Count only the final bread or sum value added at every stage.\n\nCIRCULAR FLOW: Households provide labour, land, capital and enterprise to firms; firms pay wages, rent, interest and profit. Households spend income on goods and services produced by firms. Money flows in the opposite direction to real goods and factor services. In a simple two-sector model, households' saving is a leakage and firms' investment is an injection.\n\n2. GDP, GNP/GNI, NNP AND NATIONAL INCOME\n• GDP: production within Nigeria, regardless of who owns the producing business.\n• Gross National Income (GNI), traditionally called GNP in many examination texts: GDP plus net primary/factor income from abroad. If Nigerians earn ₦40bn abroad while foreign investors earn ₦70bn in Nigeria, net factor income from abroad is −₦30bn.\n• Net National Product (NNP): GNP less depreciation (capital consumed in production).\n• National income at factor cost, in the traditional JAMB presentation: NNP at market prices − indirect taxes + subsidies.\n• Per capita income: national income ÷ population. It is an average, not proof that every citizen earns that amount.\n• Nominal GDP uses current prices; real GDP removes the effect of changes in prices. Growth comparisons should use real GDP.\n\nWORKED NATIONAL INCOME ACCOUNT (₦ BILLION)\nConsumption C = 600; investment I = 150; government purchases G = 200; exports X = 120; imports M = 170.\nGDP = C + I + G + (X − M) = 600 + 150 + 200 + 120 − 170 = 900.\nNet factor income from abroad = −30, so GNP = 900 − 30 = 870.\nDepreciation = 70, so NNP = 870 − 70 = 800.\nIndirect taxes = 60 and subsidies = 10, so national income at factor cost = 800 − 60 + 10 = ₦750bn.\nEXAM WARNING: Imported goods are subtracted because they are included in spending but were not produced domestically.\n\n3. THREE METHODS OF MEASUREMENT\nOUTPUT METHOD: Add value added by agriculture, industry and services. Example: farmer sells wheat for ₦100, miller sells flour for ₦160 and baker sells bread for ₦250. Value added = 100 + (160 − 100) + (250 − 160) = ₦250, not ₦510.\nINCOME METHOD: Add factor incomes generated by production: wages, rent, interest and profits, with appropriate accounting adjustments.\nEXPENDITURE METHOD: Add spending on domestically produced final output: C + I + G + (X − M). Investment here means spending on new capital goods and inventory changes, not merely buying existing shares.\nThe three approaches should agree in principle; statistical discrepancies can arise in actual accounts.\n\n4. WHAT COUNTS AND WHAT DOES NOT\n• A newly built house is current production; resale of an existing house is not new output, though current estate-agent services count.\n• A government teacher's salary pays for a current service; an unemployment benefit is a transfer payment and is excluded as current output.\n• Intermediate inputs are excluded to avoid double counting.\n• Unpaid household work is often excluded from measured GDP, although it has economic value.\n• Illegal or informal activity may be difficult to measure accurately; data coverage varies.\n\n5. REAL GDP, DEFLATOR AND GROWTH\nGDP deflator = (nominal GDP ÷ real GDP) × 100, when the base year index is 100.\nIf nominal GDP is ₦1,200bn and real GDP is ₦1,000bn, the deflator is 120.\nReal growth = (current real GDP − previous real GDP) ÷ previous real GDP × 100.\nIf real GDP rises from ₦800bn to ₦840bn, growth is 5%. Nominal growth alone may reflect inflation.\n\n6. CONSUMPTION, SAVING AND INVESTMENT\nDisposable income Yd = C + S. Average propensity to consume (APC) = C/Yd; average propensity to save (APS) = S/Yd; APC + APS = 1.\nMarginal propensity to consume (MPC) = change in C ÷ change in Yd. Marginal propensity to save (MPS) = change in S ÷ change in Yd. MPC + MPS = 1.\nIf disposable income rises by ₦100 and consumption by ₦75, MPC = 0.75 and MPS = 0.25.\nThe simple consumption function is C = a + bYd, where a is autonomous consumption and b is MPC. Savings can be negative when households spend more than their disposable income.\n\n7. THE SIMPLE MULTIPLIER\nIn a simplified economy with fixed prices, unused capacity and no taxes or imports, the investment multiplier k = 1/(1 − MPC) = 1/MPS.\nFor MPC = 0.8, k = 5. A ₦20bn increase in autonomous investment may produce a ₦100bn total increase in equilibrium income. In reality, taxes, imports, capacity limits and inflation reduce or complicate the effect.\nIn a two-sector equilibrium, planned S = planned I. In an open economy with government, equilibrium requires injections (I + G + X) to equal leakages (S + T + M), under the model's definitions.\n\n8. WHY NATIONAL INCOME STATISTICS MATTER\nGovernments use them for economic planning, budget forecasts, assessing sector performance and comparisons over time. However, per capita GDP does not reveal income distribution, pollution, unpaid work, leisure or the quality of public services. Purchasing power parity can improve international living-standard comparisons.\n\n9. NIGERIAN APPLICATION\nAgriculture, oil and gas, manufacturing, trade and services contribute to Nigeria's measured output. The National Bureau of Statistics (NBS) compiles national accounts. GDP rebasing changes the base-year structure and measurement coverage; it is not the same as creating new goods overnight.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\nGDP is domestic, GNP/GNI is national; gross becomes net after depreciation; market price becomes factor cost after subtracting indirect taxes and adding subsidies in the traditional formula. Do not add intermediate goods. Do not confuse transfers with production. Use real GDP for output growth.\n\nQUICK CHECK\nQ: A Nigerian-owned factory in Ghana produces goods. Is its output in Nigeria's GDP? A: No. Its factor income may affect Nigeria's GNI.\nQ: If MPC = 0.6, what is the simple multiplier? A: 1/(1 − 0.6) = 2.5.\nQ: Why can GDP rise while living standards fall? A: Population, prices, inequality, environmental damage and service quality can change independently of total output."}},{"id":"money-inflation","old":{"title":"Money and inflation","body":"Money is anything generally accepted as a means of payment and settlement of debts. Barter (exchange of goods for goods) suffered from the need for a double coincidence of wants, indivisibility, lack of a common measure of value and difficulty of storing wealth.\n\nFunctions: medium of exchange, unit of account (measure of value), store of value, standard of deferred payment.\nQualities: general acceptability, durability, portability, divisibility, homogeneity, relative scarcity, stability of value, difficulty to forge.\nTypes: commodity money (cowries, manillas), metallic coins, paper money (legal tender), bank money (cheques, demand deposits), and today electronic money (cards, transfers; the CBN's eNaira, 2021). Fiat money has value by law, not by its material. The naira replaced the Nigerian pound on 1 January 1973 at £1 = ₦2.\n\nDemand for money (Keynes's liquidity preference): transactions motive, precautionary motive (both depend on income), and speculative motive (inversely related to the interest rate).\nSupply of money: currency issued by the CBN plus bank deposits. M1 (narrow money) = currency outside banks + demand deposits; M2 (broad money) = M1 + savings and time deposits.\n\nCredit creation: banks lend out deposits beyond their cash reserve.\nCredit multiplier = 1 ÷ cash ratio. Example: a new deposit of ₦10,000 with a 10% cash ratio can lead to total deposits of ₦10,000 × (1 ÷ 0.1) = ₦100,000, of which new loans (credit created) = ₦90,000.\n\nValue of money: what a unit of money can buy; it moves inversely with the price level.\nQuantity theory (Irving Fisher): MV = PT, where M = money supply, V = velocity of circulation, P = price level, T = volume of transactions. With V and T constant, P rises in proportion to M.\nExample: M = ₦2,000, V = 5, T = 500: P = (2,000 × 5) ÷ 500 = ₦20. If M doubles to ₦4,000, P doubles to ₦40.\n\nInflation: a persistent rise in the general price level.\n• Demand-pull: excess demand (\"too much money chasing too few goods\").\n• Cost-push: rising production costs (wages, fuel, naira depreciation).\n• Imported inflation; structural inflation (bottlenecks such as poor power and roads).\n• Creeping (mild), galloping (rapid), and hyperinflation (prices out of control).\nEffects: fixed-income earners, creditors and savers lose; debtors and holders of real assets gain; exports become less competitive; uncertainty discourages investment.\nControl: tight monetary policy (higher monetary policy rate, OMO sales, higher reserve requirements), reduced government spending or higher taxes, price and wage controls, raising output.\nMeasurement: inflation rate = (CPI this year − CPI last year) ÷ CPI last year × 100. If CPI rises from 200 to 230, inflation = 30 ÷ 200 × 100 = 15%. Nigeria's inflation rose above 30% in 2024; the NBS rebased the CPI in 2025.\n\nDeflation: a persistent fall in the general price level, often with falling output and unemployment. Disinflation is a fall in the RATE of inflation (prices still rising, but more slowly).\n\nCommon traps:\n• Inflation reduces the value of money: the two move in opposite directions.\n• Debtors gain from inflation; creditors lose.\n• The speculative demand for money depends on the interest rate, not income.\n• Credit multiplier = 1 ÷ cash ratio (10% gives 10, not 0.1).\n• Disinflation is not deflation."},"new":{"title":"Money and inflation","body":"MONEY AND INFLATION — PURCHASING POWER, BANK CREDIT AND PRICE CHANGES\n\n1. WHAT IS MONEY?\nMoney is anything generally accepted as payment for goods and services and in settlement of debts. Before money, barter required a double coincidence of wants: a yam farmer wanting shoes had to find a shoemaker wanting yams at the same time. Barter also made pricing, storing wealth and dividing goods difficult.\n\nFUNCTIONS OF MONEY\n• Medium of exchange: pays for goods without direct barter.\n• Unit of account: prices and debts are expressed in a common measure.\n• Store of value: purchasing power can be transferred to the future, although inflation erodes it.\n• Standard of deferred payment: wages, loans and contracts can be settled later.\nQualities include acceptability, durability, portability, divisibility, uniformity, relative scarcity and resistance to counterfeiting.\n\n2. FORMS OF MONEY\nCommodity money has direct use or commodity value; fiat currency is accepted by law and public confidence rather than intrinsic material value. Demand deposits are bank money used through transfers and cheques. Electronic payments are a METHOD of transferring value, not necessarily a new category of money; distinguish the underlying deposit from the card or app that accesses it.\nNigeria introduced the naira in 1973, replacing the Nigerian pound at ₦2 to £1. The CBN introduced the eNaira in 2021 as a central-bank digital currency; it is not identical to a commercial bank deposit.\n\n3. DEMAND FOR MONEY: KEYNES'S LIQUIDITY PREFERENCE\nTransactions motive: cash for ordinary purchases, related to income and spending.\nPrecautionary motive: cash held against emergencies and uncertainty.\nSpeculative motive: cash held in anticipation of changes in bond prices and interest rates. In the simplified Keynesian account, speculative demand tends to be greater when interest rates are low.\nThe demand for money concerns the desire to hold money balances, not the demand for goods.\n\n4. MONEY SUPPLY AND BANK CREDIT\nMoney supply includes currency held outside banks and qualifying deposits. Narrow and broad money measures depend on the central bank's definitions; avoid treating every deposit category as identical.\nBanks accept deposits and lend part of the funds they can safely deploy. In the simplest textbook deposit-expansion model:\nMaximum deposit multiplier = 1/reserve ratio.\nWith a 10% required reserve ratio and an initial new reserve injection of ₦10,000, maximum total deposit expansion is ₦100,000; the potential increase beyond the initial deposit is ₦90,000.\nASSUMPTIONS: all loan proceeds are redeposited, banks lend the permitted balance, there is no currency leakage and the reserve ratio is binding. Actual credit creation also depends on capital, demand for loans, risk and monetary policy. The multiplier is a simplified teaching model, not a guaranteed outcome.\n\n5. VALUE OF MONEY AND THE QUANTITY THEORY\nThe value of money means its purchasing power. If the general price level doubles, one naira buys about half as much as before.\nFisher's equation of exchange is MV = PT (or MV = PY in the output version), where M is money, V its velocity, P the price level and T transactions (or Y real output). If V and T are unchanged, an increase in M is associated with a proportional increase in P in this simplified model.\nExample: M = 2,000, V = 5, T = 500. P = (2,000 × 5)/500 = 20. If M doubles while V and T remain unchanged, P becomes 40.\nJAMB CAUTION: This is conditional on the assumptions, not a claim that all inflation is caused only by money supply.\n\n6. INFLATION, DEFLATION AND DISINFLATION\nInflation is a sustained increase in the GENERAL price level, not a rise in the price of just one item. It reduces the purchasing power of money.\nDeflation is a sustained FALL in the general price level.\nDisinflation means inflation continues but its RATE falls: for example, inflation slows from 20% to 12%. Prices are still rising.\nAn inflation rate of 15% does not mean every product's price rose by exactly 15%.\n\n7. MEASURING INFLATION\nThe Consumer Price Index (CPI) tracks changes in the cost of a representative basket of consumer goods and services, using expenditure weights.\nInflation rate = (current CPI − previous CPI)/previous CPI × 100.\nIf CPI rises from 200 to 230, inflation = 30/200 × 100 = 15%.\nIf CPI next rises from 230 to 253, inflation is 10% for that next period. This is disinflation relative to the earlier 15%, although the price level is higher.\nIndex numbers depend on basket composition, weights and base period. Rebasing the CPI changes the reference framework; it does not itself prove prices have fallen.\n\n8. CAUSES OF INFLATION\nDemand-pull: aggregate spending grows faster than available output, bidding up prices.\nCost-push: costs of fuel, imported inputs, electricity, wages or transport rise and firms pass some increases on.\nImported inflation: foreign price rises or currency depreciation make imported goods dearer.\nStructural pressures: poor roads, insecurity, weak electricity and supply bottlenecks limit production.\nDifferent causes may operate together. A harvest failure can raise food prices even without a surge in money supply.\n\n9. EFFECTS AND CONTROL\nUnexpected inflation harms people on fixed nominal incomes and holders of cash savings. Borrowers with fixed nominal repayments may benefit relative to lenders, though loan rates and contracts matter. High inflation can discourage long-term investment and planning.\nContractionary monetary policy may raise the policy rate, sell securities or tighten reserve requirements, reducing spending pressures. Fiscal restraint may reduce demand. Supply-side measures, such as better power and transport, can relieve cost pressures. A policy aimed at demand-pull inflation may be less effective against a severe supply shock.\n\n10. NIGERIAN APPLICATION\nExchange-rate changes, food distribution costs, petrol prices and harvest conditions can influence Nigeria's inflation. The CBN manages monetary policy; the NBS publishes price statistics. Distinguish the policy authority from the statistics agency.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\nInflation is general and sustained. Disinflation is not deflation. The value of money moves inversely with the price level. Bank lending is not unlimited. Interest rates influence speculative money demand. A 10% reserve ratio gives a theoretical multiplier of 10, not 0.1.\n\nQUICK CHECK\nQ: CPI increases from 160 to 184. Inflation? A: (24/160) × 100 = 15%.\nQ: Inflation slows from 15% to 8%. What happened? A: Disinflation.\nQ: A trader accepts a transfer for goods. Which function of money? A: Medium of exchange."}},{"id":"financial-institutions","old":{"title":"Financial institutions and monetary policy","body":"Financial institutions bring together savers and borrowers.\n\nCentral Bank of Nigeria (CBN): established by the CBN Act 1958, began operations on 1 July 1959; headquarters Abuja.\nFunctions: issues currency (naira notes and coins, printed by the Nigerian Security Printing and Minting Company), banker to government, banker to banks (lender of last resort), manages external reserves and the exchange rate, controls money supply and credit (monetary policy), supervises banks, manages public debt instruments with the Debt Management Office (DMO), and promotes development finance.\n\nCommercial banks: accept deposits (current, savings, fixed), give loans and overdrafts, create credit, provide payment services (cheques, cards, transfers), foreign exchange, safe custody, advice. Bank consolidation in 2005 raised minimum capital to ₦25bn and cut 89 banks to 25. A recapitalisation from 2024 set new minimums of ₦500bn (international licence), ₦200bn (national) and ₦50bn (regional).\nMerchant banks: wholesale banking: medium and long-term loans to companies, issuing houses, acceptance of bills, project finance (they do not take small savings deposits).\nDevelopment banks: long-term finance for development: NIDB (1964) and NBCI (1973), merged with NERFUND into the Bank of Industry (BOI, 2001); NACB (1973), merged with the People's Bank in 2000 into NACRDB, renamed the Bank of Agriculture in 2010; Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (1977) for housing. Microfinance banks (policy 2005) replaced community banks.\n\nMoney market: market for SHORT-TERM funds (up to one year). Instruments: Treasury bills, call money, commercial papers, certificates of deposit, bankers' acceptances. Institutions: CBN, commercial and merchant banks, discount houses.\nCapital market: market for LONG-TERM funds. Instruments: shares (equities), bonds, debentures. The Nigerian Stock Exchange (founded 1960 as the Lagos Stock Exchange, renamed 1977) was demutualised in 2021 into the Nigerian Exchange Group (NGX). The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC, 1979) regulates the capital market (Investments and Securities Act). Primary market = new issues; secondary market = trading in existing securities.\n\nOther institutions: insurance companies (regulated by NAICOM, 1997), pension fund administrators (PenCom, Pension Reform Act 2004, now 2014), building societies, finance houses. The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC, 1988) insures bank deposits up to a limit and helps manage failed banks.\n\nInstruments of monetary policy:\n• Monetary Policy Rate (MPR): the CBN's benchmark interest rate (introduced 2006, replacing the minimum rediscount rate). Raising it makes borrowing dearer (contractionary).\n• Open market operations: selling securities reduces money supply; buying increases it.\n• Cash reserve ratio and liquidity ratio: higher ratios reduce banks' ability to lend.\n• Special deposits, selective credit control, moral suasion, direct credit ceilings.\nContractionary (tight) policy fights inflation; expansionary (easy) policy fights recession.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Treasury bills are money-market (short-term); bonds and shares are capital-market (long-term).\n• The CBN does not accept deposits from the public; it is the bankers' bank.\n• Selling securities in OMO REDUCES money supply.\n• NDIC insures deposits; SEC regulates the capital market; NAICOM regulates insurance.\n• A higher cash reserve ratio reduces credit creation."},"new":{"title":"Financial institutions and monetary policy","body":"FINANCIAL INSTITUTIONS AND MONETARY POLICY — WHO DOES WHAT?\n\n1. THE FINANCIAL SYSTEM\nFinancial institutions connect people who save with people and organisations that need funds. They mobilise deposits, assess risk, facilitate payments, provide credit and help allocate capital. Some are deposit-taking banks; others are insurance companies, pension institutions, capital-market intermediaries or specialised development lenders.\n\n2. CENTRAL BANK OF NIGERIA (CBN)\nThe CBN was established under the 1958 Act and began operations in 1959. It is Nigeria's monetary authority and bankers' bank.\nKey functions: issuing and managing currency; banking for the federal government; holding and managing external reserves; providing liquidity support to banks as lender of last resort; implementing monetary policy; supporting payment-system stability; and supervising institutions within its regulatory remit.\nThe CBN is NOT an ordinary commercial bank where the general public opens retail savings accounts.\n\n3. COMMERCIAL BANKS\nCommercial banks accept qualifying deposits, provide payment accounts, lend to households and businesses, offer overdrafts and trade finance, and facilitate transfers. Banks earn income partly from the spread between lending rates and funding costs, fees and other permitted services.\nDEPOSITS: current accounts facilitate frequent payments; savings accounts are used to accumulate funds; fixed/term deposits are held for agreed periods.\nLOANS VS OVERDRAFTS: a loan usually provides a defined sum with repayment terms; an overdraft permits an account holder to borrow up to an agreed limit.\nBanks face credit risk, liquidity risk, operational risk and regulatory requirements.\n\n4. SPECIALISED FINANCIAL INSTITUTIONS\nMerchant banks focus on corporate and wholesale finance, investment-related services and larger transactions, subject to licence terms.\nMicrofinance banks provide services to low-income households, microenterprises and small businesses.\nDevelopment finance institutions help fund sectors or projects underserved by ordinary commercial credit. Nigerian examples include the Bank of Industry (BOI), the Bank of Agriculture and the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria.\nInsurance companies pool specified risks in exchange for premiums. Pension institutions manage retirement savings within regulatory rules.\nDo not confuse a development bank's purpose with a central bank's monetary-policy role.\n\n5. MONEY MARKET AND CAPITAL MARKET\nMONEY MARKET: short-term borrowing and lending, typically maturities up to one year. Examples include Treasury bills, call money, bankers' acceptances and commercial paper.\nCAPITAL MARKET: long-term finance, including shares and longer-term bonds.\nPRIMARY MARKET: newly issued securities raise money for the issuer.\nSECONDARY MARKET: investors trade existing securities; the issuing company normally does not receive proceeds from each resale.\nExample: a company sells newly issued shares to raise ₦1bn: primary market. An investor later sells those shares on an exchange: secondary market.\nThe Nigerian Exchange (NGX) provides a securities-trading venue; the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) regulates the capital market.\n\n6. WHO REGULATES WHAT?\nCBN: banking and monetary policy within its statutory powers.\nNDIC: deposit insurance and related bank-resolution functions.\nSEC: capital-market regulation.\nNAICOM: insurance regulation.\nPenCom: pension-sector regulation.\nJAMB TRAP: NDIC does not set the Monetary Policy Rate, and the SEC does not issue naira notes.\n\n7. MONETARY POLICY: MEANING AND OBJECTIVES\nMonetary policy is the use of central-bank instruments to influence monetary and financial conditions, particularly inflation and economic stability.\nEXPANSIONARY policy tends to lower borrowing costs or increase liquidity, supporting spending and output when demand is weak.\nCONTRACTIONARY policy tends to raise borrowing costs or reduce liquidity to restrain excessive demand and inflation.\nTrade-off: tighter credit may slow investment and employment in the short run.\n\n8. MAIN POLICY INSTRUMENTS\nMONETARY POLICY RATE (MPR): the benchmark policy rate. An increase generally pushes market borrowing costs upward, though the transmission is not always immediate or one-for-one.\nOPEN MARKET OPERATIONS (OMO): when the central bank sells eligible securities to banks and investors, it absorbs liquidity; when it buys, it injects liquidity.\nCASH RESERVE RATIO (CRR): the share of qualifying deposits banks must hold as reserves under applicable rules. Raising it usually restricts funds available for lending.\nLIQUIDITY REQUIREMENTS: require banks to hold sufficient liquid assets.\nMORAL SUASION: persuasion and guidance by the central bank.\nSELECTIVE CREDIT CONTROLS: targeted restrictions or priorities for lending.\nExample: if the CBN wants to curb excessive credit growth, it may raise the MPR or CRR, or sell securities.\n\n9. MONETARY POLICY TRANSMISSION\nA higher policy rate may increase commercial lending rates, reducing some household borrowing and business investment. Lower spending can reduce demand pressures and, over time, inflation. Exchange rates, expectations, banking conditions and supply shocks can weaken or delay the effect.\nA lower policy rate may encourage credit, but banks will not necessarily lend if borrowers are too risky.\n\n10. FINANCIAL STABILITY AND DEPOSIT INSURANCE\nA solvent bank has assets sufficient to meet its liabilities; a liquid bank can meet obligations when due. A bank can face liquidity problems even when its assets exceed its liabilities. Deposit insurance protects eligible deposits up to applicable limits; it is not a promise that every balance of any size is fully insured.\n\n11. NIGERIAN EXAM CONTEXT\nThe CBN's historical consolidation and later recapitalisation initiatives sought to strengthen bank capital and resilience. Licensing requirements can change, so exam answers should distinguish the underlying functions from changing numerical thresholds. Treasury bills belong to the money market; long-term government bonds belong to the capital market.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\nA higher CRR generally reduces credit capacity. Selling securities drains liquidity. The CBN is lender of last resort, not an ordinary retail lender. A share is ownership; a bond is debt. Primary markets fund new issues; secondary markets provide trading and liquidity.\n\nQUICK CHECK\nQ: Which institution issues naira currency? A: CBN.\nQ: A six-month Treasury bill belongs to which market? A: Money market.\nQ: What happens when the central bank sells securities? A: It normally withdraws liquidity.\nQ: Which body regulates insurance? A: NAICOM."}},{"id":"public-finance","old":{"title":"Public finance","body":"Public finance is the study of how government raises revenue, spends it and manages its debt.\n\nSources of government revenue in Nigeria: taxes, oil revenue (petroleum profits tax, royalties, NNPC Limited dividends), rents, fees and fines, licences, grants, profits of public enterprises, and borrowing.\n\nTaxation: a compulsory levy by government.\n• Direct taxes: paid directly by the person taxed and cannot be shifted: personal income tax (PAYE), companies income tax, petroleum profits tax, capital gains tax.\n• Indirect taxes: on goods and services; can be shifted to consumers: VAT, excise duties, import and export duties.\nVAT was introduced in 1993 at 5% and raised to 7.5% in 2020. Under the tax reform laws signed in 2025 (effective 2026), the Federal Inland Revenue Service became the Nigeria Revenue Service.\n\nTax systems:\n• Progressive: rate rises with income. Example: 10% on ₦100,000 = ₦10,000; 20% on ₦1,000,000 = ₦200,000.\n• Proportional: same rate for all (10% of ₦100,000 = ₦10,000; 10% of ₦1,000,000 = ₦100,000).\n• Regressive: takes a larger share of a low income than a high one (a flat ₦5,000 levy is 5% of ₦100,000 but only 0.5% of ₦1,000,000).\n\nCanons (principles) of taxation (Adam Smith): equity (according to ability to pay), certainty, convenience, economy (cheap to collect). Others: flexibility, productivity, simplicity, neutrality.\nIncidence: the final burden of a tax. Impact is where it is first imposed. Incidence depends on elasticities (see the elasticity lesson).\nEffects of taxation: on work effort, savings, investment, prices and income distribution. Tax evasion is illegal; tax avoidance uses legal loopholes.\n\nGovernment expenditure: recurrent (salaries, overheads, debt servicing) and capital (roads, dams, schools). Reasons: provide public and merit goods, defence and security, redistribution, development.\n\nBudget: an annual statement of planned revenue and expenditure, presented by the President and passed by the National Assembly as the Appropriation Act.\n• Surplus budget: revenue > expenditure (to fight inflation).\n• Deficit budget: expenditure > revenue (to stimulate the economy; financed by borrowing).\n• Balanced budget: revenue = expenditure.\n\nFiscal policy: use of taxation and spending to influence the economy; expansionary (lower taxes, more spending) or contractionary.\n\nPublic debt: internal (bonds, Treasury bills) and external (World Bank, IMF, Eurobonds). The Debt Management Office (DMO, 2000) manages it. In 2005 the Paris Club wrote off about $18bn of Nigeria's debt.\n\nRevenue allocation: sharing of federally collected revenue (the Federation Account) among the federal, state and local governments (vertical) and among states (horizontal). Principles: derivation, population, equality of states, need, land mass, internal revenue effort. Past commissions: Phillipson (1946), Hicks-Phillipson (1951), Chick (1953), Raisman (1958), Binns (1964), Dina (1968), Aboyade (1977), Okigbo (1980). Today the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC, 1989) advises; the current shares are Federal 52.68%, States 26.72%, Local governments 20.60%, after 13% derivation to oil-producing states (1999 Constitution).\n\nCommon traps:\n• VAT is an INDIRECT tax even though it is collected by government agencies.\n• A flat-rate percentage is proportional, not regressive.\n• Impact (who pays first) differs from incidence (who finally bears it).\n• Deficit budgets are expansionary; surplus budgets are contractionary.\n• Debt servicing is recurrent expenditure."},"new":{"title":"Public finance","body":"PUBLIC FINANCE — TAXES, BUDGETS, DEBT AND REVENUE SHARING\n\n1. MEANING AND SCOPE\nPublic finance studies how government raises money, spends it, borrows and manages public resources. Its objectives include providing public services, promoting economic stability, redistributing income and supporting development.\nPublic finance is broader than taxation: budgets, debt, public expenditure and intergovernmental revenue sharing are also central.\n\n2. SOURCES OF GOVERNMENT REVENUE\nTax revenue includes personal income tax, company income tax, value-added tax, excise duties and customs duties. Non-tax revenue includes royalties, licences, fees, fines, dividends from government investments and some public-enterprise earnings. Grants are transfers received; borrowing finances spending but creates a liability and should not be confused with ordinary earned revenue.\nNigeria's public revenue can be affected by oil production, world oil prices, non-oil tax compliance and exchange-rate movements.\n\n3. TAXATION: DEFINITION AND CLASSIFICATION\nA tax is a compulsory payment imposed by government under law, generally without a direct, specific benefit in return for each payment.\nDIRECT TAXES are assessed on income, profits or property of the person legally liable, such as personal income tax and companies income tax.\nINDIRECT TAXES are imposed on transactions or goods and services, such as VAT and excise duties. The economic burden may be passed through prices.\nCAUTION: The legal classification of a tax and its economic incidence are not identical.\n\n4. PROGRESSIVE, PROPORTIONAL AND REGRESSIVE TAXATION\nPROGRESSIVE: the average tax rate rises as taxable income rises.\nPROPORTIONAL: the same percentage rate applies across income levels.\nREGRESSIVE: the average tax burden as a percentage of income falls as income rises.\nEXAMPLE: Under a flat 10% income tax, ₦100,000 income pays ₦10,000 and ₦1,000,000 pays ₦100,000: proportional.\nA fixed ₦5,000 levy is 5% of ₦100,000 but 0.5% of ₦1,000,000: regressive relative to income.\nJAMB TRAP: A flat amount and a flat percentage are different. A fixed amount is not the same as a proportional tax.\n\n5. TAX IMPACT AND TAX INCIDENCE\nIMPACT: the person or business initially required to pay or remit the tax.\nINCIDENCE: who ultimately bears the economic burden after price and wage adjustments.\nFor a tax on a product, consumers generally bear more when their demand is relatively inelastic; sellers bear more when supply is relatively inelastic. Real-world outcomes depend on both curves and market conditions.\nExample: if a fuel tax raises the pump price, motorists may bear part of it even when a seller formally remits the tax.\nTax evasion illegally conceals or misreports tax obligations; lawful tax planning follows applicable law. Do not describe all tax minimisation as illegal.\n\n6. CANONS OF TAXATION\nAdam Smith's classic principles:\nEQUITY: fairness according to an appropriate ability-to-pay principle.\nCERTAINTY: taxpayers know what, when and how to pay.\nCONVENIENCE: collection occurs in a practical manner.\nECONOMY: collection costs should be low relative to revenue.\nModern policy also considers simplicity, administrative feasibility, efficiency and responsiveness to economic conditions.\n\n7. GOVERNMENT EXPENDITURE\nRECURRENT expenditure supports ongoing operations: salaries, routine maintenance, overheads and debt-service payments.\nCAPITAL expenditure finances long-lived assets and projects such as roads, hospitals, water systems and school buildings.\nA teacher's salary is recurrent; constructing a new school is capital. Buying equipment may be capital, depending on budget classifications.\nSpending can improve welfare and productivity but also cause waste or inflation if poorly targeted or financed.\n\n8. GOVERNMENT BUDGETS\nA budget is a statement of planned government revenue and expenditure for a financial period.\nBALANCED: revenue equals expenditure.\nSURPLUS: revenue exceeds expenditure.\nDEFICIT: expenditure exceeds revenue.\nWORKED EXAMPLE: projected revenue ₦850bn, planned expenditure ₦1,000bn. Deficit = ₦150bn. Government may finance it through borrowing or other lawful financing arrangements.\nA deficit is not automatically good or bad: effects depend on the state of the economy, spending quality, financing costs and debt sustainability.\n\n9. FISCAL POLICY\nFiscal policy uses government spending and taxation to influence economic activity.\nEXPANSIONARY: increased spending or lower taxes, all else equal, may raise aggregate demand and employment.\nCONTRACTIONARY: reduced spending or higher taxes, all else equal, may restrain aggregate demand and inflation.\nAutomatic stabilisers, such as income-tax collections that change with earnings, can moderate cycles without a new law each time.\nFiscal policy is conducted through government budgets; monetary policy is conducted by the central bank.\n\n10. PUBLIC DEBT\nINTERNAL debt is owed to domestic creditors; EXTERNAL debt is owed to foreign creditors. Governments borrow through instruments such as Treasury bills, bonds and external loans.\nDebt servicing includes interest payments and other contractual obligations; repayment of principal reduces outstanding debt.\nA debt-to-GDP ratio compares the debt stock with annual output, but sustainability also depends on revenue, interest rates, maturity, currency risk and the ability to refinance.\n\n11. FEDERAL REVENUE ALLOCATION\nVertical allocation divides eligible federally collected revenue among levels of government; horizontal allocation divides a state's or local government's share among units at that level.\nPrinciples can include derivation, population, equality, needs and internal revenue effort.\nThe Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) advises on revenue allocation under Nigerian law. Students should distinguish the allocation PRINCIPLES from numerical formulas, which can change.\nDerivation is associated with revenue sourced from producing areas, including constitutionally relevant provisions for natural resources.\n\n12. NIGERIAN CONTEXT AND EXAM PRECISION\nTax institutions and laws can be restructured; learn the functions of tax administration as well as historical names. A value-added tax is an indirect tax. Borrowing is a financing source, not the same as tax revenue. A public budget is a plan, not a guarantee that all funds will be spent.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\nImpact is initial legal payment; incidence is ultimate economic burden. A proportional tax has a constant percentage, not a constant amount. Deficit means spending exceeds revenue. A road project is typically capital expenditure; routine wages are recurrent. Fiscal policy is not monetary policy.\n\nQUICK CHECK\nQ: Government earns ₦500bn and spends ₦650bn. Budget position? A: ₦150bn deficit.\nQ: A 10% tax at all income levels is what type? A: Proportional.\nQ: Who ultimately bears a tax? A: The person on whom its incidence falls."}},{"id":"growth-development","old":{"title":"Economic growth, development and planning","body":"Economic growth is a sustained increase in a country's real output (real GDP or real GDP per capita) over time. Economic development is wider: growth plus structural change and improved welfare: less poverty, unemployment and inequality, better health, education, housing and freedom. A country can grow without developing if the gains go to a few.\n\nMeasures and indicators: real GDP growth rate, per capita income, Human Development Index (HDI: health (life expectancy), education (years of schooling), income (GNI per capita); 0 to 1; published by the UNDP since 1990), life expectancy, literacy, infant mortality, access to clean water and power, poverty rate, Gini coefficient (from the Lorenz curve: 0 = perfect equality, 1 = perfect inequality).\n\nCharacteristics of developing countries (Nigeria): low per capita income, dependence on primary products (crude oil), high population growth, unemployment and underemployment, low savings and investment, poor infrastructure, low technology, dual economy, high external debt, income inequality. Problems include corruption, political instability, insecurity, poor power supply and brain drain.\n\nDevelopment planning: a deliberate effort by government to set targets and allocate resources to achieve them over a period.\nTypes: perspective (long-term), medium-term (rolling) and short-term (annual budget); comprehensive or partial; imperative or indicative.\nNigeria's plans:\n• Colonial Ten-Year Plan of Development and Welfare (1946–1956).\n• First National Development Plan 1962–1968.\n• Second NDP 1970–1974 (reconstruction after the civil war).\n• Third NDP 1975–1980 (largest, funded by the oil boom).\n• Fourth NDP 1981–1985.\n• Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP), July 1986: devaluation through the Second-tier Foreign Exchange Market (SFEM), deregulation of interest rates, privatisation and commercialisation, abolition of commodity boards, trade liberalisation, reduced subsidies.\n• Three-year rolling plans from 1990; Vision 2010 (1996); NEEDS (National Economic Empowerment and Development Strategy, 2004); Vision 20:2020 (2009); Economic Recovery and Growth Plan (ERGP, 2017–2020); National Development Plan 2021–2025; Agenda 2050 (long-term).\n• Global goals: Millennium Development Goals (2000–2015) were replaced by the Sustainable Development Goals (2015–2030).\n\nProblems of planning in Nigeria: unreliable data, inadequate finance, dependence on oil revenue, political instability and frequent policy changes, corruption, poor implementation, lack of skilled manpower, over-ambitious targets.\n\nGrowth rate example: real GDP rises from ₦400bn to ₦420bn: growth rate = (420 − 400) ÷ 400 × 100 = 5%. If population grows by 3%, per capita growth is about 5 − 3 = 2%.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Growth is quantitative (output); development is qualitative (welfare).\n• Use REAL GDP, not nominal, for growth.\n• The First NDP began in 1962, not 1960.\n• SAP began in 1986 under Babangida; NEEDS came in 2004.\n• A high Gini coefficient means MORE inequality."},"new":{"title":"Economic growth, development and planning","body":"ECONOMIC GROWTH, DEVELOPMENT AND PLANNING — BEYOND GDP\n\n1. GROWTH VERSUS DEVELOPMENT\nECONOMIC GROWTH is a sustained increase in an economy's REAL output over time, usually measured by real GDP or real GDP per person.\nECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT is broader: improvements in living standards and capabilities, including health, education, productive employment, poverty reduction, infrastructure, security and economic opportunities.\nA country can record rising real GDP while many households remain poor if population rises quickly or benefits are concentrated among a small group.\n\n2. MEASURING ECONOMIC GROWTH\nReal GDP growth rate = (real GDP this year − real GDP last year)/real GDP last year × 100.\nIf real GDP increases from ₦400bn to ₦420bn, growth is (20/400) × 100 = 5%.\nIf population rises by 3%, growth in real output per person is approximately 2%; the exact ratio is 1.05/1.03 − 1 ≈ 1.94%.\nWHY REAL GDP? Nominal GDP may rise simply because prices rise. Real GDP adjusts for price changes.\nPer capita real GDP is useful but does not show inequality, unpaid work or environmental damage.\n\n3. DEVELOPMENT INDICATORS\nHuman Development Index (HDI): combines life expectancy, education and income indicators. The UNDP publishes HDI values; higher values indicate stronger measured human development.\nLife expectancy: average expected years of life under current mortality patterns.\nLiteracy and school attainment: indicators of education and human capital.\nInfant and child mortality: measures of health conditions.\nAccess to safe water, electricity and sanitation: basic welfare and infrastructure.\nPoverty measures: identify the extent and depth of deprivation.\nEmployment indicators: distinguish unemployment from underemployment.\nNo single indicator captures all dimensions of development.\n\n4. INCOME INEQUALITY AND THE LORENZ CURVE\nThe Lorenz curve plots the cumulative percentage of income received against the cumulative percentage of the population, ranked from poorest to richest.\nThe 45-degree equality line means, for example, the poorest 40% receive 40% of total income.\nA Lorenz curve farther below the equality line indicates greater inequality.\nThe Gini coefficient summarises inequality: 0 means perfect equality; 1 means maximum inequality under the usual normalisation.\nWORKED INTERPRETATION: if the poorest 50% of households receive only 20% of income, distribution is unequal. This does not automatically reveal whether average income is high or low.\n\n5. FEATURES AND CHALLENGES OF DEVELOPING ECONOMIES\nPossible characteristics include low productivity, infrastructure gaps, reliance on primary commodity exports, limited industrial diversification, underemployment, unequal access to services and vulnerability to external shocks.\nThese are tendencies, not universal descriptions of every developing country.\nIn Nigeria, electricity supply, transport costs, insecurity, access to finance and skills shortages can constrain production and investment.\nHuman capital development, functioning institutions and technological adoption can raise long-run productivity.\n\n6. ECONOMIC GROWTH FACTORS\nCapital accumulation: more or better machines, roads and equipment.\nHuman capital: education, training and health improve labour productivity.\nTechnology: improved methods increase output from given inputs.\nInstitutions: predictable rules, secure property rights and effective public administration encourage productive activity.\nNatural resources can provide revenue, but dependence on a single export can create volatility.\nInvestment must be productive; merely spending more does not guarantee sustained growth.\n\n7. ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT STRATEGIES\nAgricultural modernisation can raise yields, food security and rural incomes.\nIndustrialisation can create jobs, diversify exports and increase value added.\nInfrastructure investment can reduce transport and power costs.\nEducation, health and digital skills strengthen human capital.\nFinancial inclusion can help productive firms obtain savings and credit services.\nDiversification reduces dependence on one commodity or sector.\nTrade policy choices involve benefits and costs; protection without competitiveness can raise prices and reduce efficiency.\n\n8. DEVELOPMENT PLANNING\nDevelopment planning is a deliberate process of setting economic and social objectives, identifying resources and choosing actions and timelines.\nSHORT-TERM plans cover immediate priorities, often annual budgets.\nMEDIUM-TERM plans coordinate priorities over several years.\nLONG-TERM or perspective plans establish broad goals over a decade or longer.\nINDICATIVE planning guides private and public actors through targets and incentives; IMPERATIVE planning relies more on binding directives.\nA good plan needs reliable data, realistic funding, accountability and measurable outcomes.\n\n9. NIGERIA'S PLANNING HISTORY — EXAM TIMELINE\nColonial Ten-Year Plan of Development and Welfare: 1946–1956.\nFirst National Development Plan: 1962–1968.\nSecond National Development Plan: 1970–1974, associated with post-civil-war reconstruction.\nThird National Development Plan: 1975–1980, during the oil-boom era.\nFourth National Development Plan: 1981–1985.\nStructural Adjustment Programme (SAP): introduced in 1986, with exchange-rate, trade and public-sector reforms.\nNEEDS: National Economic Empowerment and Development Strategy, introduced in 2004.\nEconomic Recovery and Growth Plan (ERGP): 2017–2020.\nNational Development Plan: 2021–2025.\nNigeria Agenda 2050: longer-term planning framework.\nGLOBAL GOALS: Millennium Development Goals covered 2000–2015; Sustainable Development Goals cover 2015–2030.\nBe careful with dates: the first post-independence national plan started in 1962, not 1960.\n\n10. WHY PLANS FAIL\nWeak or outdated statistics make targets unreliable.\nInsufficient or volatile finance can delay projects.\nPolitical changes can interrupt implementation.\nCorruption, procurement delays and poor coordination can waste resources.\nUnrealistic assumptions and inadequate monitoring can conceal failure.\nGood plans require budgets, clear responsibilities, performance measures and periodic review.\n\n11. GROWTH, DEVELOPMENT AND SUSTAINABILITY\nSustainable development aims to improve present welfare without undermining future generations' ability to meet their needs. Pollution, land degradation and depletion of resources can impose long-term costs even when current output rises.\nA productive economy needs both output growth and improvements in how its benefits are shared.\n\nJAMB TRAPS\nGrowth is about real output; development includes welfare. A higher Gini means more inequality. An outward PPC shift can represent greater productive capacity. Do not confuse the HDI with GDP alone. SAP began in 1986; NEEDS began in 2004. Real GDP per person can stagnate even if total GDP rises.\n\nQUICK CHECK\nQ: Real GDP rises 5%, population 7%. What happens to output per person? A: It falls, approximately 2%.\nQ: What does a Lorenz curve farther from the equality line mean? A: Greater inequality.\nQ: Is a higher nominal GDP proof of growth? A: No, inflation may account for the increase."}}];
